  { id:'opqb-t9-349', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'Which **laser** is used for **capsulotomy**?',
    options:['Diode laser','Carbon dioxide laser','Excimer laser','YAG laser'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"YAG laser capsulotomy **is used to treat posterior capsule opacification after cataract surgery**\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The deck names the laser and the target in one line.** `L18) Cataract II`, postoperative complications: \"Posterior Capsule Opacification (PCO) — Months–years. **Most common late complication.** Lens epithelial cells migrate on the posterior capsule → 'after-cataract'. Presents as gradual return of visual blurring. **Tx: Nd:YAG laser capsulotomy** — outpatient, definitive, excellent outcomes.\" The same deck's rapid-fire revision page asks it as a one-liner: \"Q: Most common post-op complication? A: **PCO — treated by Nd:YAG laser capsulotomy**.\"\n\n**Why Nd:YAG and not a thermal laser.** The Nd:YAG is a *photodisruptive* laser: it delivers nanosecond pulses that ionise a tiny volume of tissue and blow a mechanical hole in it, so it can cut a membrane that sits **behind** a clear IOL without being absorbed by pigment and without heating anything on the way in. That is exactly what a capsulotomy needs — a clean central opening in the opacified posterior capsule, made without opening the eye. This bank asks the clinical half of the same fact at `opqb-t9-380`.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What that laser is actually for |\n|---|---|\n| **Diode laser** | An infrared *photocoagulating* laser — heat, absorbed by pigment. Used for cyclophotocoagulation (ablating ciliary body in refractory glaucoma) and for retinal photocoagulation, including retinopathy of prematurity. It would be absorbed by the iris and retina, not by a clear capsule. |\n| **Carbon dioxide laser** | A far-infrared cutting/vaporising laser absorbed by **water**, so it is stopped dead by the tear film and cornea. Its ophthalmic use is oculoplastic — skin and lid surgery — never intraocular. |\n| **Excimer laser** | The ultraviolet *photoablative* laser of corneal refractive surgery — PRK, LASIK, PTK. `L15,16) Cornea` gives it to the cornea only, and this bank has already made the same point at `opqb-t8-319`, where PRK, LASIK and PTK all put laser on the cornea. It cannot reach the capsule. |\n\n**Note on wording:** the stem says \"capsulotomy\" without qualification. In cataract practice the word covers two different things — the **anterior** capsulotomy/capsulorhexis made by the surgeon during the operation (`L18` calls it \"Continuous curvilinear capsulorhexis (CCC) — anterior capsule opening\"), and the **posterior** capsulotomy made by laser afterwards. Only the second is a laser procedure, which is what makes the question answerable.",
    objective:'Nd:YAG is the laser of posterior capsulotomy for PCO; diode, CO2 and excimer lasers all act elsewhere in or around the eye.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.54 (Q349), key p.62 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-350', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'**Unilateral aphakia** is likely to be corrected by any of the following **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Anterior chamber intraocular lens','Posterior chamber intraocular lens','Contact tens','Glasses'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**`L17) Cataract I` prints the whole answer as a three-row table, and the row that matters is the first one.** Under \"Optical rehabilitation of aphakia (glasses, contact lenses, IOLs)\":\n\n| Correction | Binocular aphakia | **Monocular** aphakia |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Glasses** | suitable | **least suitable** (image magnification **~30 %** → anisokonia → binocular diplopia) |\n| **Contact lenses** | suitable | **suitable** (magnification ~7–10 % → minimal anisokonia → no binocular diplopia) |\n| **IOL** | — | **most physiological** (magnification ~3 % → no anisokonia) |\n\n**The number is the reason.** A +10 to +12 D aphakic spectacle sits ~12 mm in front of the eye, and the further a plus lens is from the nodal point the larger the retinal image it forms — about **30 %** larger. One eye seeing an image a third bigger than the other cannot be fused, so the patient gets **aniseikonia** (the decks spell it \"anisokonia\") and intractable binocular diplopia. In *bilateral* aphakia both images are magnified equally, there is nothing to fuse them against, and thick aphakic spectacles are perfectly usable — which is why the answer turns entirely on the word **unilateral** in the stem.\n\n**A contact lens sits on the cornea**, only ~3 mm from the nodal point, so it magnifies by ~7–10 % — inside the ~5 % that most people can fuse, or close enough to it. **An IOL sits in the posterior chamber**, essentially at the plane of the lens it replaced, so it magnifies by ~3 % and the patient is optically almost normal. `L18) Cataract II` adds the spectacle's other defects: \"Aphakic spectacles (+10–12 D: **thick, minifying, ring scotoma**)\".\n\n**Why the other three are all workable in a unilateral case:**\n\n| Option | Why it corrects unilateral aphakia |\n|---|---|\n| **Anterior chamber intraocular lens** | The fallback when there is **no capsular support** — `L18` \"When no capsular support. Angle-supported or iris-fixated (Artisan® lens)\", and it is exactly what is done after an ICCE. Not the first choice, but it corrects the aphakia. This bank keys it as the right answer at `opqb-t9-367`. |\n| **Posterior chamber intraocular lens** | The ideal — in the capsular bag, most physiological, ~3 % magnification. See `opqb-t9-354`. |\n| **Contact tens** | Printed \"tens\" for *lens*. A contact lens is the classical non-surgical answer for a monocular aphake; only intolerance defeats it, and then a secondary IOL is offered — the exact chain this bank builds at `opqb-t9-401`. |\n\n*(The ring scotoma, the +10 to +12 D figure and the aniseikonia threshold are printed; the 12 mm vertex distance and the nodal-point explanation of why a distant plus lens magnifies are **not taken from the course material** and are added to make the 30 % figure make sense.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Spectacles are the one correction that fails in UNILATERAL aphakia, because their ~30 % image magnification cannot be fused with the fellow eye.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.54 (Q350), key p.62' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-351', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'**Phakolytic glaucoma** is best treated by:',
    options:['Fistulizing operation','Cataract extraction','Cyclo-destructive procedure','Miotics and Beta blockers'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Take the lens out, because the lens is the disease.** `L17) Cataract I` builds the mechanism a step at a time under \"Complications → Lens induced glaucoma → Phacolytic\": \"**Degeneration of lens capsule → leakage of lens proteins →** immune reaction in anterior chamber (uveitis) → uveitic glaucoma\" and \"**clogging of TM by lens proteins macromolecules → secondary open angle glaucoma (trabecular)**\". `L18) Cataract II` states the same in its summary row — \"Phacolytic Glaucoma · Hypermature/mature · **Liquefied cortex leaks proteins** · HM, pain, redness · **Macrophages block TM** · **Urgent lens removal**\" — and lists it among the **URGENT / MANDATORY** indications for cataract surgery: \"Phacolytic glaucoma — leaking lens proteins → macrophage-induced raised IOP\".\n\n**So the trabecular meshwork here is anatomically open and structurally intact; it is simply choked with protein and protein-laden macrophages.** Remove the source and the outflow recovers. Every other option treats the *pressure* while leaving a leaking hypermature lens inside the eye, which means the protein keeps coming.\n\n**In practice the pressure is knocked down medically first** — that is how you clear a hazy cornea enough to operate safely — but the definitive treatment, and what the question asks for, is lens extraction. This bank asks the other half of the same fact at `opqb-t9-405` (which cataract does it occur in) and the phacomorphic counterpart at `opqb-t9-404`.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it is not the treatment |\n|---|---|\n| **Fistulizing operation** | A trabeculectomy makes a new drainage route past a diseased meshwork. Here the meshwork is not diseased, it is blocked by material that has a removable source — and operating on an eye full of lens protein invites the new fistula to block too. |\n| **Cyclo-destructive procedure** | Ablating ciliary body to cut aqueous production is a salvage procedure for refractory glaucoma, usually in an eye with poor visual potential. A phacolytic eye has an entirely recoverable retina behind an opaque lens; destroying its ciliary body would be the wrong operation on a curable eye. |\n| **Miotics and Beta blockers** | Temporising only. A beta blocker reduces aqueous production and buys hours; a **miotic** is actively unhelpful here, because pilocarpine increases the blood–aqueous barrier breakdown in an eye that is already inflamed and does nothing about protein in the meshwork. |\n\n*(That miotics are specifically unhelpful in an inflamed lens-induced glaucoma is **not taken from the course material** — the decks name only the mechanism and the urgent removal.)*\n\n**Spelling note:** the bank prints \"Phakolytic\" here and \"Phacolytic\" at `opqb-t9-405`. Both are the same entity.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Phacolytic glaucoma is cured by removing the leaking lens; pressure-lowering drops or drainage surgery leave the protein source in place.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.54 (Q351), key p.62' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-352', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'**Lens induced glaucoma** is **least likely** to occur in:',
    options:['Intumescent cataract.','Anterior lens dislocation,','Posterior subcapsular cataract','Posterior lens dislocation'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**A lens raises the pressure by being too big, by leaking, or by being in the wrong place. A posterior subcapsular cataract is none of those things.**\n\n`L17) Cataract I` gives the two classical lens-induced glaucomas, and both need a change in the lens's *bulk* or its *integrity*:\n\n| Type | Mechanism, as `L17` prints it |\n|---|---|\n| **Phacomorphic** | \"**Intumescence** (lens fibres fluid imbibition) → **increased lens thickness** → **pupil block** → elevated IOP\" |\n| **Phacolytic** | \"Degeneration of lens capsule → **leakage of lens proteins** → … clogging of TM by lens proteins macromolecules → secondary open angle glaucoma\" |\n\n**A PSC is a thin plaque of granular opacity on the back surface of the lens.** `L18) Cataract II` describes it as an \"Opacity at posterior capsule (nodal point location)\", a \"Granular posterior capsule plaque\" that is \"**Most visually disabling per unit size**\". Per unit size — that is the point. It ruins vision precisely because so little of it does so much damage, sitting on the visual axis at the nodal point. The lens around it keeps its normal thickness, its capsule stays intact, and its zonules are untouched, so there is nothing to block the pupil and nothing to leak into the anterior chamber.\n\n**Why the other three do cause it:**\n\n| Option | Route to raised IOP |\n|---|---|\n| **Intumescent cataract** | The textbook phacomorphic mechanism above — a swollen lens pushes the iris forward and shuts the angle. `L18` lists \"Phacomorphic glaucoma — swollen lens causing angle closure\" as an **urgent** indication for surgery, and this bank builds the vignette at `opqb-t9-404`. |\n| **Anterior lens dislocation** | The lens in the anterior chamber blocks the pupil directly and jams the angle mechanically — the fastest and most complete of all the lens-induced pressure rises. `L18` names \"Subluxated lens with acute pupillary block\" among the urgent indications. |\n| **Posterior lens dislocation** | Slower and less certain, but real: the lens in the vitreous can induce a phacolytic-type reaction as its capsule degenerates, and the eye is at risk of secondary glaucoma from the accompanying vitreous disturbance. `opqb-t9-358` in this same topic keys **uveitis and neovascular glaucoma** as complications of posterior dislocation. |\n\n*(The detail that a posteriorly dislocated lens causes glaucoma late and by protein leakage rather than by pupil block is **not taken from the course material**; the decks name anterior dislocation and intumescence explicitly and leave the posterior case to the complications list this bank prints at Q358.)*\n\n**Read the stem carefully:** it asks for the **least likely**, not the impossible. All four can end in raised pressure in some eye somewhere; only one has no mechanism of its own.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Lens-induced glaucoma needs a swollen, leaking or displaced lens; a posterior subcapsular cataract changes neither lens volume, capsule integrity nor lens position.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.54 (Q352), key p.62' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-353', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'**Earliest visual rehabilitation** occurs with:',
    options:['Phacoemulsification plus intraocular lens implantation','Intracapsular cataract extraction plus intraocular lens implantation','Extracapsular cataract extraction plus intraocular lens implantation','Small incision cataract extraction'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"Phacoemulsification plus IOL implantation: **Gives earliest visual rehabilitation due to small incision, rapid healing, and immediate visual recovery.**\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The whole answer is the size of the wound.** `L18) Cataract II` lays the three techniques side by side and the incision is the first row of each:\n\n| Technique | Incision | Wound closure | Consequence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Phacoemulsification** | \"**2.2 – 3.0 mm** (clear corneal or scleral tunnel)\" | \"**Self-sealing incision — NO sutures needed → less astigmatism**\" | \"**Gold standard worldwide** for routine cataract surgery\" |\n| **ECCE** | \"**8 – 10 mm** limbal incision\" | \"Requires **10/0 nylon wound sutures → significant astigmatism**\" | Rigid PMMA IOL, slower stabilisation |\n| **ICCE** | \"**Large incision** — entire lens + capsule removed\" | — | \"Historical procedure — rarely performed today\" |\n\n**A wound that seals itself has no sutures to pull the cornea out of shape and none to remove later**, so the refraction is stable within days rather than months. A 10 mm sutured limbal wound flattens the cornea in the meridian of the sutures, and the astigmatism it induces keeps changing as the sutures loosen and the wound remodels — the patient sees, but cannot be given final glasses, for weeks. That gap between *seeing* and *being rehabilitated* is what the question is asking about. This bank asks the same fact from the astigmatism side at `opqb-t9-381`.\n\n**Why the other three are slower:**\n\n| Option | Why not earliest |\n|---|---|\n| **Intracapsular cataract extraction plus IOL** | The largest wound of the three, and the capsule is gone, so the IOL has to go in the anterior chamber — more induced astigmatism, more endothelial risk, and `L18` lists its complications as \"bullous keratopathy, glaucoma, vitreous prolapse\". Slowest of all. |\n| **Extracapsular cataract extraction plus IOL** | Excellent final vision, but through a sutured 8–10 mm wound with \"significant astigmatism\" that must settle first. |\n| **Small incision cataract extraction** | A genuinely small, usually sutureless manual tunnel — the closest rival, and much faster than ECCE. It is beaten because it still delivers a rigid nucleus through a wound several millimetres wider than a phaco incision, and because the option as printed does **not** include an IOL, so the eye it leaves is aphakic and needs optical correction before it is rehabilitated at all. |\n\n*(That the printed option D omits the IOL is worth noticing rather than glossing: `L18` gives phaco a \"**FOLDABLE** acrylic IOL — fits through 2.2 mm incision\", which is what keeps the phaco incision small enough to self-seal.)*",
    objective:'Phaco with a foldable IOL rehabilitates fastest because a 2.2–3.0 mm self-sealing wound induces little astigmatism and needs no sutures.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.54 (Q353), key p.62 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-354', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'**Best site** where **intraocular lens** is fitted:',
    options:['Capsular ligament','Endo sulcus','Ciliary supported','Capsular bag'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"The ideal site for IOL implantation is the **capsular bag for stability and centration.**\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**`L18) Cataract II` says the same in six words.** Under IOL types \"By POSITION\": \"**Posterior Chamber IOL (PCIOL)** — **In the capsular bag (preferred)** or ciliary sulcus. **Most physiological position.**\"\n\n**Why the bag and nothing else.** The capsular bag is the space the natural lens occupied, so an IOL sitting in it sits at the plane the eye's optics were built around — which is why `L17) Cataract I` measures its image magnification at only **~3 %** and calls the IOL \"**Most physiological**\". The bag is a closed elastic envelope suspended by the zonules, so it holds the optic centred on the visual axis and stops it tilting or decentring. Everything that makes phacoemulsification the standard operation exists to preserve that bag: `L18` lists \"**Continuous curvilinear capsulorhexis (CCC)** — anterior capsule opening\", \"Hydrodissection separates cortex from capsule\" and \"Aspiration of cortex → **IOL implantation into capsular bag**\", and the point of ECCE leaving \"Posterior capsule **LEFT IN PLACE** (supports IOL)\" is the same.\n\n**The price of the bag is the one complication it guarantees:** lens epithelial cells left on that capsule migrate across it and opacify it — posterior capsule opacification, \"the most common late complication\" (`L18`), asked in this topic at `opqb-t9-349`, `opqb-t9-365` and `opqb-t9-380`. It is a fair trade, because YAG capsulotomy fixes it in an outpatient clinic.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| **Capsular ligament** | Not a structure that holds an IOL. The zonules — sometimes called the suspensory ligament of the lens — are threads running from ciliary processes to the equatorial capsule (`L17`: \"suspended in place by the **zonular fibres** (attached to the ciliary processes)\"; `L18`: \"**Zonules** from ciliary processes → equatorial capsule\"). They suspend the bag; nothing is implanted into them. |\n| **Endo sulcus** | A mangling of the **ciliary sulcus**, and `L18` does list the sulcus — but as the alternative, not the preferred site: \"In the capsular bag (**preferred**) or ciliary sulcus.\" A sulcus-fixated IOL is what you fall back to when the posterior capsule tears. |\n| **Ciliary supported** | The same fallback described another way, and the same reason it loses: support from the ciliary sulcus rather than from within the bag allows decentration and tilt, and rubs the posterior iris surface. |\n\n**Note that options B and C name essentially the same non-preferred site**, which makes the question easier than it looks: if two options are the same answer, neither can be the single best one.",
    objective:'The capsular bag is the preferred IOL site — most physiological plane, best stability and centration; the ciliary sulcus is only the fallback.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.54 (Q354), key p.62 (explanation box printed)' }

,

  { id:'opqb-t9-355', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'**After 48 hours** of cataract extraction operation, a patient complained of **ocular pain and visual loss**. On examination, this eye looked **red with ciliary injection, corneal oedema and absent red reflex**. The **first suspicion** must be:',
    options:['Secondary glaucoma.','Anterior uveitis.','Bacterial endophthalmitis.','Acute conjunctivitis'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"Postoperative red eye, pain, corneal edema, absent red reflex → **suspect endophthalmitis first**.\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**⚠️ THE BANK PRINTS THIS QUESTION TWICE — and the second printing MOVES THE KEY LETTER.** Book p.60 (Q392) prints the same stem word for word, bar \"oedema\"/\"edema\", with the same four options **REORDERED**, so the printed key is **C** here and **B** there. **Same answer, different letter** — which is exactly why a key letter is never the thing to memorise. The two printings are held here as ONE entry, per the within-bank reprint rule: reordered options are not replaced options. This page prints the explanation box; p.60 prints none.\n\n**Every element of the vignette is on the deck's endophthalmitis panel.** `L18) Cataract II`, postoperative complications: \"**Endophthalmitis** — **24–72 hrs** (acute) or weeks (delayed). **Most serious vision-threatening complication.** Causative agents: **CoNS (Staphylococcus epidermidis most common)**, S. aureus, streptococci. Signs: **pain, redness, reduced VA, hypopyon, yellowish vitreous haze.** Tx: **urgent vitreous tap + intravitreal antibiotics (vancomycin + ceftazidime)**. Severe: vitrectomy.\" Forty-eight hours falls inside the 24–72 hour window exactly.\n\n**The single most useful sign in the stem is the ABSENT RED REFLEX.** The lens has been removed, so there is no lens opacity left to block the reflex; the only thing that can still swallow it is something filling the vitreous cavity. \"Yellowish vitreous haze\" is what that something is. A red eye with pain after surgery has many causes; a red painful eye with **nothing coming back from the fundus** is an infected vitreous until proved otherwise.\n\n**\"First suspicion\" is doing real work in the stem.** It is not asking what is most likely on a balance of numbers — postoperative uveitis and a pressure spike are both commoner than endophthalmitis. It is asking what you must exclude first, because it is the one diagnosis on the list where a few hours' delay costs the eye. `L18` calls it \"the most serious vision-threatening complication\", and this bank asks that ranking directly at `opqb-t9-391`.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| **Secondary glaucoma** | A pressure spike after surgery (retained viscoelastic, inflammatory debris) does give pain, redness and corneal oedema — `L18` lists \"Also: retained viscoelastic, **raised IOP**\" under corneal decompensation. But the media stay clear enough for a red reflex, and it is not the diagnosis that has to be excluded within hours. |\n| **Anterior uveitis** | Expected to some degree after every cataract operation, and it explains the ciliary injection. It does not explain an **absent** red reflex, and treating a sterile post-op uveitis with steroids while an infection is brewing is precisely the mistake this question exists to prevent. |\n| **Acute conjunctivitis** | Wrong pattern of redness. Conjunctivitis gives superficial, peripheral, fornix-maximal injection with discharge and **no** ciliary injection, **no** corneal oedema, **no** loss of the red reflex and no visual loss. |\n\n*(That endophthalmitis presents with a hypopyon in most but not all cases, and that the red reflex is the discriminator once the lens is out, is a reading of the deck's own sign list rather than a separate printed rule.)*",
    objective:'A red, painful eye with corneal oedema and an absent red reflex 24–72 hours after cataract surgery is bacterial endophthalmitis until excluded.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.54 (Q355), key p.62 (explanation box printed); reprinted with reordered options as Q392 on p.60' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-356', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-white',
    stem:'**Leukocoria** is **NOT** present in:',
    options:['Congenital Cataract.','Congenital Glaucoma.','Retinoblastoma.','Retinopathy of prematurity.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Leukocoria is a WHITE PUPIL, and the white has to come from behind or within the pupil. Congenital glaucoma whitens the CORNEA instead — in front of the pupil — so the pupil itself is not white.**\n\n`L33,34) The White Eye` prints the differential as a list, and congenital glaucoma is not on it: \"**Leukocoria** → Congenital cataract, RB, ROP, Coats, PFV, Toxoplasma, Toxocara, retinal dysplasia, etc\", and again \"Leukocoria → '**White pupil in a child**' … Most dangerous → **Retinoblastoma** (malignant tumour of the photoreceptors)\". The same deck organises the whole white-eye problem by **where** the white is, and the two levels are kept apart deliberately: \"Pupil → Aperture (Membrane) / **Lens (Cataract)** / **Retro-pupil (Leukocoria)**\" — that is, at the lens or behind it.\n\n**What congenital glaucoma actually shows.** `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` lists its signs as \"**Corneal oedema, Haab's stria** · **Enlarged corneal diameter** · Broad limbus · Increased IOP\". A buphthalmic eye is hazy and grey-white **across the whole cornea**, not just in the pupillary aperture, and the haze is oedema in a stretched cornea with breaks in Descemet's membrane. Held at arm's length, the eye looks big and steamy — not like a white dot in the middle of a normal-looking eye. Both are white-eye emergencies in a child; they are simply different signs.\n\n**Why the other three do produce leukocoria:**\n\n| Option | Where the white comes from |\n|---|---|\n| **Congenital Cataract.** | The opaque lens itself, directly in the pupil. `content\\ophtho\\book\\ch16-paediatric.txt` (printed 242–312): \"A common presentation for **total** congenital cataract is **leukocoria** (white colour of the pupil) or altered red reflex\", and `L17) Cataract I` lists \"In children → **leukocoria** ('white pupil') (D.Dx of leukocoria)\" among the signs of cataract. |\n| **Retinoblastoma.** | The white tumour mass itself, reflecting light back through the pupil. `L18) Cataract II`'s revision page: \"Q: Leukocoria first-exclude? A: **RETINOBLASTOMA** — malignant, requires urgent B-scan + MRI.\" The most dangerous member of the list, which is why it is never the answer to a *not present* question. |\n| **Retinopathy of prematurity.** | Advanced (stage 5) ROP ends in a retrolental fibrovascular mass and total detachment, which presents as a white pupil. It is on `L33,34`'s printed list by name. |\n\n**The clinical rule the question is built on:** any white pupil in a child is retinoblastoma until proved otherwise, and the first test is a red reflex examination — which is exactly what would be *normal in the pupil but abnormal in the cornea* in congenital glaucoma.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Congenital glaucoma clouds the cornea, not the pupil; congenital cataract, retinoblastoma and ROP all fill the pupillary aperture with white.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.54 (Q356), key p.62' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-357', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'**Symptoms of early senile cataract** include',
    options:['Pain and headache','Transient diminution of vision','Uniocular diplopia','Floating opacities'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**⚠️ THE PRINTED KEY IS B, AND IT DIVERGES FROM BOTH CACHED DECKS. It is recorded here exactly as the bank prints it, and the divergence is set out below rather than disputed — the `answer` field is always the bank's key.**\n\n**What the decks actually print as the early symptoms.** `L17) Cataract I`, clinical picture: \"Symptoms ('**Gradual progressive painless diminution of vision**') · Diminution of vision ('foggy vision') · **Glare** ('defective vision with bright illumination beside visual targets') · **Defective colour vision** (colour desaturation / different perceptions) · **Defective vision in different illumination conditions** (dark / light)\". `L18) Cataract II` gives a longer list under \"Senile Cataract — Symptoms & Signs\": \"Gradual progressive visual loss — **Painless — most common complaint**\", \"**Glare** — Difficulty in bright light / oncoming headlights (PSC worst)\", \"**Duskiness**\", \"**Halos around lights** — Due to prismatic effect of opaque fibres\", \"**Monocular diplopia** — Multiple images with one eye (cortical or nuclear)\", \"'**Second sight**' — Nuclear: myopic shift allows reading without glasses (temporary)\".\n\n**So option C, \"Uniocular diplopia\", is printed as a cataract symptom in `L18` under the name monocular diplopia**, and it is an *early* one, because it depends on the lens having irregular zones of differing refractive index — which requires the lens to be only partly opacified. In a mature cataract nothing gets through to be doubled. `L18` even lists it as a surgical indication: \"**Monocular diplopia interfering with daily activities**\". It is what makes cataract the answer to a monocular double image, since a true binocular diplopia would disappear on covering one eye.\n\n**Option B, \"Transient diminution of vision\", is not printed anywhere in either deck as a cataract symptom, and the defining adjective in both decks is the opposite of transient**: `L17` calls cataract \"Gradual **progressive** painless diminution of vision\" and `L18` \"Gradual **progressive** visual loss\". Transient visual loss is the language of amaurosis fugax, of papilloedema's obscurations, of angle-closure attacks — of things that come and go. A lens opacity does not come and go.\n\n**The most defensible reading of the printed key** is that it is meant as the fluctuation of vision *with illumination* — `L17`'s \"Defective vision in different illumination conditions (dark / light)\", where a patient with a central opacity sees better in dim light with a wide pupil and worse in sun with a small one, and reports it as vision that \"comes and goes\". That is a real early complaint. But it is not what the words \"transient diminution of vision\" ordinarily mean, and the option that the decks print verbatim is C. **Learn C as the deck's answer and B as this bank's key.**\n\n**Why the other two are wrong, and both are wrong for the same structural reason — cataract is painless and intraocular:**\n\n| Option | Where that symptom belongs |\n|---|---|\n| **Pain and headache** | Not cataract. Both decks stress \"**painless**\". Pain in a cataractous eye means a lens-induced complication has supervened — phacomorphic or phacolytic glaucoma (`opqb-t9-404`, `opqb-t9-405`) — or a different disease entirely. |\n| **Floating opacities** | Vitreous, not lens. Floaters move with eye movement and drift when it stops, because they are suspended in a gel; a lens opacity is fixed in the visual axis and does not float. Floaters point to posterior vitreous detachment, vitreous haemorrhage or vitritis. |\n\n*(That monocular diplopia requires a partly opacified lens, and that floaters localise to the vitreous by their movement, are **not taken from the course material** — the decks name both symptoms without explaining the mechanism.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The decks print glare, colour desaturation, halos, monocular diplopia and second sight as early cataract symptoms; this bank keys "transient diminution of vision" instead.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.54 (Q357), key p.62 — key diverges from L17 and L18; see the explanation' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-358', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'**Posterior dislocation of the lens** is characterized by:',
    options:['Loss of one porkioge-Saoson','Uveitis may occur as complication','Neovascular glaucoma is a frequent complication','B&C'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The key is D, so both B and C are being asserted as true, and option A is being asserted as false.**\n\n**Option A is a garbled printing of the PURKINJE-SANSON images**, and it is worth unscrambling because the underlying sign is real and is taught. \"porkioge-Saoson\" is what the page prints — verified on a 2.2× crop — for *Purkinje-Sanson*. Four catoptric images are reflected by the eye's four refracting surfaces: **I** the anterior cornea, **II** the posterior cornea, **III** the **anterior lens capsule**, and **IV** the **posterior lens capsule** (image IV is inverted, the other three erect). `L17) Cataract I` uses the third of them as a sign of cataract: among the signs it lists \"Lens opacity (**absent 3rd Purkinje image**, slit lamp)\".\n\n**If the lens leaves the pupillary area entirely, images III and IV both disappear — TWO are lost, not one.** That is why option A, as printed, is not the characteristic finding: a posteriorly dislocated lens does not cost you \"one\" Purkinje image. It also means the finding is not specific to *posterior* dislocation, since an anteriorly dislocated or absent lens does the same thing. `L18) Cataract II` makes the mirror-image point about a pseudophakic eye: \"**Purkinje reflexes from IOL surfaces**\" are visible when an implant occupies the plane the lens used to.\n\n**Option B — uveitis.** A dislocated lens sitting in the vitreous has a capsule that degenerates over time, and lens protein escaping into the eye provokes exactly the reaction `L17` describes in the phacolytic pathway: \"leakage of lens proteins → **immune reaction in anterior chamber (uveitis)**\". `L18` names the entity: \"**Phacoanaphylactic uveitis** — lens protein hypersensitivity\", listed among the **urgent** indications for lens removal, and \"**Lens-induced uveitis** unresponsive to medical therapy\" beside it. A lens in the wrong place is a slow-release antigen depot.\n\n**Option C — neovascular glaucoma.** New iris vessels grow in response to **retinal ischaemia**. A lens dropped into the vitreous is not itself ischaemic, but the events that surround it are: `L18` lists \"Posterior capsule rupture + vitreous loss (intraop) → **higher risk of retinal detachment**\" and \"**Dropped nucleus** → vitrectomy required\", and a chronically detached or repeatedly operated retina is an ischaemic retina. **Rubeosis and neovascular glaucoma follow ischaemia, not the lens directly** — so calling it a *frequent* complication, as the option does, is the bank stating it more strongly than either deck does. The key nonetheless includes it.\n\n*(The four Purkinje-Sanson images, their surfaces and which one is inverted are **not taken from the course material** — `L17` names only the third — and the ischaemic pathway to rubeosis is likewise supplied, since neither cataract deck teaches neovascular glaucoma. `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` is where the module teaches rubeosis.)*\n\n**Compare `opqb-t9-352`**, which asks the same anatomy from the pressure side: anterior dislocation blocks the pupil at once, posterior dislocation causes trouble slowly and by other routes.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A posteriorly dislocated lens is complicated by lens-protein uveitis and, through the ischaemia that accompanies it, neovascular glaucoma; it removes TWO Purkinje images, not one.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.54 (Q358), key p.62 — option A printed "Loss of one porkioge-Saoson"' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-359', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'If a patient present in your office with a **mature cataract**, which test would **NOT** be helpful in deciding whether the patient should have **cataract surgery**?',
    options:['Ultrasound','Color vision testing','Two-point discrimination','Fluorescein angiography'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**⚠️ THE PRINTED KEY IS C, AND `L17) Cataract I` LISTS TWO-POINT DISCRIMINATION AMONG THE TESTS DONE IN CATARACT. The key is recorded as printed and the divergence is set out here, not disputed.**\n\n**What the decks print about testing behind an opaque lens.** The whole difficulty of a mature cataract is that you cannot see the retina, so every pre-operative test is a way of asking the retina a question it can answer through white glass. `L17` lists, under the signs of cataract: \"**Tests → intact 2-point discrimination, normal pupil reaction**\". `L18) Cataract II` builds the same idea into its pre-operative checklist: \"**Projection of light** in all 4 directions (dense cataract)\", \"**Color discrimination (red vs green)**\", \"**Potential acuity measurement (PAM)** if doubt about macular function\", \"**Pupillary reflexes** (relative afferent pupillary defect?)\", and under signs, \"**Color discrimination** — Red vs green test = **good retinal function**\" and \"**Projection of light** — Good in all 4 directions = **good prognosis**\".\n\n**Two-point discrimination belongs to that family.** It asks the patient to say whether two closely separated light sources held in front of the eye are one light or two; the ability to separate them requires a working macula, and it survives a dense media opacity because it needs light localisation rather than image formation. `L17` calls it *intact* in uncomplicated cataract, i.e. it is a test whose normal result reassures you. On the deck's own terms it is therefore **helpful**.\n\n**The option that is genuinely useless here is D, fluorescein angiography** — and saying so is not disputing the key, it is explaining what the alternatives do. FFA photographs dye passing through the retinal circulation, which requires a clear enough media for a camera to focus on the fundus. Through a mature cataract there is no fundus view at all, so there is nothing to photograph. `L18` never lists FFA in the pre-operative work-up; the deck's answer to \"the fundus cannot be seen\" is \"**B-scan ultrasound if fundus not visible → rule out retinal detachment / tumour**\" and \"**Electroretinogram (ERG) if macular disease suspected**\". This bank keys ultrasound for exactly that reason at `opqb-t9-376` and electrophysiology at `opqb-t9-377` and `opqb-t9-385`, and it keys FFA as the *wrong* answer at `opqb-t9-376`.\n\n**A defensible reading of the printed key** is that two-point discrimination is a crude bedside test whose result does not change the surgical decision — you would operate on a mature cataract with a normal pupil reaction and good light projection whatever it showed — whereas ultrasound and colour testing can each stop or modify the plan. That is an argument about *decisiveness*, not about whether the test works. It is recorded here because the key requires some account, but `L17`'s own sentence is the stronger evidence and it points the other way.\n\n**Why the other two are helpful:**\n\n| Option | What it contributes |\n|---|---|\n| **Ultrasound** | The single most important test through an opaque media — \"B-scan ultrasound if fundus not visible → **rule out retinal detachment / tumour**\". It can turn a routine operation into a contraindicated one. |\n| **Color vision testing** | \"**Red vs green test = good retinal function**\" — a cheap macular-function probe that works through a dense cataract, because it needs only enough light to excite cones, not a focused image. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Through a mature cataract the useful tests are those that work without a fundus view — B-scan, light projection, colour discrimination, ERG; angiography needs a clear media.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.55 (Q359), key p.62 — key diverges from L17:227; see the explanation' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-360', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'The **myopic change** in the refractive state of the eye in **nuclear sclerosis** can be attributed to:',
    options:['Increase in the curvature of the lens','Anterior displacement of the iris lens diaphragm','Increase in the refractive index of the nucleus','All of the above'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The deck names one of the three mechanisms explicitly and the key takes all three.**\n\n`L18) Cataract II`, nuclear cataract panel: \"Exaggeration of nuclear sclerosis (yellowing → brunescence) · **Increased nuclear refractive index → acquired myopia** · Interferes predominantly with **distant** vision · '**Second sight**': elderly regain near vision temporarily\", with the margin note \"**:nuclear cataract → myopia, not hyperopia**\". Its revision page repeats it: \"Q: Nuclear cataract → which vision affected most? A: **DISTANT vision (myopic shift)**. Patient may read better ('second sight')\".\n\n**Why an index change alone produces myopia.** The dioptric power of a refracting surface is (n₂ − n₁)/r. If the nucleus becomes optically denser, n₂ rises while the radius r is unchanged, and the lens becomes a stronger plus lens without changing shape at all. A stronger lens in an eye of fixed axial length brings the focus **in front of** the retina — myopia. This is the mechanism the deck teaches, and it alone is sufficient.\n\n**The other two mechanisms named in the options are real but secondary**, and this is where the key's \"all of the above\" is doing work the decks do not do:\n\n| Option | The mechanism |\n|---|---|\n| **Increase in the curvature of the lens** | A cataractous lens imbibes fluid and swells — `L17) Cataract I` calls it \"**Intumescence** (lens fibres liquefaction and fluid imbibition → **swelling of lens**)\". A swollen lens is steeper (smaller radius of curvature) as well as thicker, and a steeper surface is a stronger one, adding plus power and therefore myopia. |\n| **Anterior displacement of the iris lens diaphragm** | The same swelling pushes the whole lens–iris diaphragm forward — `L17` traces it to \"**increased lens thickness → pupil block** → elevated IOP\" in phacomorphic glaucoma. Moving a plus lens forward, away from the retina and towards the corneal plane, increases the effective plus power of the eye, again a myopic shift. |\n| **Increase in the refractive index of the nucleus** | The deck's own printed mechanism, above. |\n\n**So all three push the same way**, which is what makes \"All of the above\" defensible even though only the third is taught. The clue in the stem is \"nuclear sclerosis\" specifically: index change is what *sclerosis* means, and it is the one that operates even in a lens that has not swollen at all.\n\n**This is the most heavily reprinted idea in the topic.** The same shift is asked as a mechanism here, as a refraction change at `opqb-t9-362`, as a vignette in a 60-year-old at `opqb-t9-402`, as a driver who can suddenly read at `opqb-t9-398`, and again at `opqb-t9-407`; the existing question `opqb-t1-70` asks it as \"second sight\".\n\n*(That a forward-shifted plus lens has greater effective power, and the (n₂ − n₁)/r derivation, are **not taken from the course material** — `L18` states the index mechanism as a result, without the optics.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Nuclear sclerosis causes a myopic shift chiefly by raising the nuclear refractive index; lens steepening and forward displacement of the lens–iris diaphragm add to it.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.55 (Q360), key p.62' }

,

  { id:'opqb-t9-361', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'Cataract can be removed by all of the following techniques **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Pars plana lensectomy.','Phacoemulsification.','Phacodonesis.','Aspiration irrigation'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Phacodonesis is a SIGN, not an operation.** The suffix is *-donesis*, trembling: it is the visible wobble of a lens whose zonules have given way, seen when the patient moves the eye and the lens quivers a moment behind it. It tells you the lens is subluxated and warns the surgeon that the capsular bag may not hold an IOL — `L18) Cataract II` lists \"subluxated lenses\" among the cases still sent for ECCE rather than phaco, and \"Still performed in **Morgagnian cataract with zonular weakness**\" among the surviving indications for ICCE. Finding phacodonesis changes the operation; it is not one.\n\n**The three that are operations:**\n\n| Option | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| **Pars plana lensectomy.** | The lens is removed from behind, through the pars plana, with a vitreous cutter — the approach used for paediatric cataract, for a lens dropped into the vitreous, and where an anterior approach is unsafe. |\n| **Phacoemulsification.** | `L18`: \"Incision 2.2 – 3.0 mm … **Ultrasonic probe emulsifies nucleus into fragments** (divide & conquer, stop & chop, phaco chop) … **Gold standard worldwide** for routine cataract surgery.\" |\n| **Aspiration irrigation** | `L17) Cataract I` gives it by age: \"Surgery (Lens extraction) — **Children → irrigation / aspiration (soft lens)** · Adults → conventional ECCE, small incision cataract extraction (phacoemulsification).\" A child's lens has no hard nucleus, so it can simply be washed out; no ultrasound is needed. |\n\n**The trap is that all four words look surgical.** Three end in a procedure noun (*-ectomy*, *-emulsification*, *aspiration*); the fourth ends in a clinical-sign noun. Two other members of the same family are worth keeping beside it: **iridodonesis**, the tremulous iris of an aphakic eye (`L18` lists it among the classic signs of aphakia — \"**Iridodonesis** (tremulous iris); deep anterior chamber; jet-black (dark) pupil\"), and phacodonesis itself, which is iridodonesis's cause when the lens is still present.\n\n*(The etymology, the mechanism of the wobble and the pars plana route are **not taken from the course material** — `L18` names subluxation and zonular weakness without defining phacodonesis, and neither deck names lensectomy.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Phacodonesis is the tremulousness of a subluxated lens — a sign of zonular weakness, not a method of removing a cataract.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.55 (Q361), key p.62' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-362', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'**Early nuclear cataract** can change the refraction:',
    options:['From plus 2 to plus 4.','From plus 2 to emmetropia.','From minus 6 to minus 4.','From minus 2 to emmetropia'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**⚠️ THE PRINTED KEY IS C, AND IT IS A HYPEROPIC SHIFT IN A QUESTION ABOUT NUCLEAR CATARACT. The key is recorded exactly as the bank prints it, per the project rule that a defective key is noted and never disputed; what follows sets out the arithmetic and the deck's own statement.**\n\n**What the decks say the shift must be.** `L18) Cataract II`, nuclear cataract: \"**Increased nuclear refractive index → acquired myopia**\", and in the margin of the same slide, in as many words, \"**:nuclear cataract → myopia, not hyperopia**\". Its revision page: \"Q: Nuclear cataract → which vision affected most? **A: DISTANT vision (myopic shift)**.\" `opqb-t9-360` in this topic keys the mechanism, `opqb-t9-398` and `opqb-t9-402` key vignettes of it, and `opqb-t9-398`'s own printed box says \"**Myopic shift in emmetropes is caused by nuclear sclerosis**\".\n\n**Now read the four options as arithmetic. A myopic shift means the refraction moves in the MINUS direction.**\n\n| Option | Start → end | Direction of the change | Myopic shift? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **A. From plus 2 to plus 4.** | +2 → +4 | **+2 D more plus** | No — hyperopic shift |\n| **B. From plus 2 to emmetropia.** | +2 → 0 | **2 D less plus** | **Yes** — a 2 D myopic shift |\n| **C. From minus 6 to minus 4.** | −6 → −4 | **2 D less minus** | No — hyperopic shift |\n| **D. From minus 2 to emmetropia** | −2 → 0 | 2 D less minus | No — hyperopic shift |\n\n**Only option B moves the eye in the myopic direction**, and it is the classical presentation: a hyperope who has needed reading glasses for twenty years finds he no longer does, because his own lens is now supplying the plus power his spectacles used to. That is \"**second sight**\" (`L18`: \"Nuclear: **myopic shift allows reading without glasses** (temporary)\"), and it is the same eye that then loses distance vision.\n\n**Options C and D describe the opposite phenomenon** — a myope becoming less myopic — which is what happens in an eye that is *losing* plus power, not gaining it. The commonest cause of that in practice is **aphakia** (`L18`: \"+10 to +12 D hypermetropia (previously emmetropic eye)\") or a posteriorly dislocated lens, both of which take the lens out of the optical path.\n\n**A charitable reading of the printed key** would be that the writer intended the numbers on the *number line* rather than the sign, reading \"−6 to −4\" as a change of magnitude 2 without attending to direction. Nothing in the page settles it; the printing is unambiguous and the key letter on book p.62 is a bare \"C\" with no explanation box.\n\n**What to carry into the exam:** the fact is *early nuclear cataract shifts refraction towards myopia*, and the option that says so on this page is B.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Nuclear cataract shifts refraction in the MYOPIC direction; of the four printed options only "+2 to emmetropia" does that, though the bank keys "−6 to −4".',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.55 (Q362), key p.62 — key diverges from L18:145 and L18:161; see the explanation' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-363', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'**Phacoemulsification** is **NOT** the method of choice for treating:',
    options:['Immature senile cataract.','Mature senile cataract.','Anteriorly dislocated lens.','Complicated cataract'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Phacoemulsification needs three things: a lens still held by its zonules, a capsular bag to work inside, and a nucleus soft enough to emulsify. An anteriorly dislocated lens has lost the first two.**\n\nA phaco tip sculpts and chops a nucleus while the capsular bag holds it steady; the bag is opened by a **continuous curvilinear capsulorhexis** and then used as a container for the ultrasound energy and afterwards for the IOL (`L18) Cataract II`: \"CCC — anterior capsule opening … Aspiration of cortex → **IOL implantation into capsular bag**\"). A lens that has fallen forward into the anterior chamber is loose in the eye, sitting against the corneal endothelium, with no zonular support to steady it and no safe way to keep the fragments where they belong. Emulsifying it there would grind ultrasound energy directly against the endothelium.\n\n**What is done instead is to remove the lens whole, in its capsule** — the intracapsular operation, which is why `L18` keeps ICCE alive for exactly these cases: \"**ENTIRE lens including capsule removed** (cryo or forceps)\", \"No capsular bag → IOL placed in **anterior chamber (ACIOL)**\", \"Still performed in Morgagnian cataract with **zonular weakness** (careful selection)\". This bank keys that directly at `opqb-t9-394`, whose printed box reads \"Intracapsular extraction **is required when lens is dislocated**\", and `opqb-t9-400` keys ECCE + IOL for a hard nuclear cataract.\n\n**Why the other three are all phaco cases:**\n\n| Option | Why phaco suits it |\n|---|---|\n| **Immature senile cataract.** | The easiest case there is — a partly opacified lens with an intact capsule, normal zonules and a nucleus of ordinary hardness. `L18` calls phaco the \"**Gold standard worldwide for routine cataract surgery**\". |\n| **Mature senile cataract.** | Harder — a white lens gives no red reflex to stain the rhexis against and the nucleus is dense — but still routine phaco with trypan blue and a careful technique. Only a **grade 4–5 black** nucleus sends the surgeon back to ECCE, which is why `opqb-t9-386` keys intumescent, not black, cataract as the phaco case. |\n| **Complicated cataract** | Cataract secondary to ocular disease — `L17) Cataract I` lists \"High myopia, Anterior uveitis, Topical steroids\" locally and \"Diabetes, Cushing syndrome, Hyperthyroidism\" systemically, and `L18`'s summary row gives complicated cataract the morphology \"Usually **PSC**\" with \"Posterior synechiae (uveitis)\". Phaco is the operation; the extra work is breaking synechiae and dilating a stiff pupil, not changing technique. |\n\n**The whole family of surgical-choice questions in this topic turns on one axis** — *is the capsule intact and are the zonules holding?* If yes, phaco. If the nucleus is rock hard but the bag is intact, ECCE. If the lens is loose or dislocated, ICCE or lensectomy.\n\n*(That mature cataracts are stained with trypan blue, and the grade 4–5 threshold, are **not taken from the course material**; `L18` says only that ECCE is \"Still used for very hard (grade 4–5) nuclei, subluxated lenses, resource-limited settings\".)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Phaco requires intact zonules and a capsular bag; a dislocated lens is removed intracapsularly or by lensectomy instead.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.55 (Q363), key p.62' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-364', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'**Management of infantile cataract**:',
    options:['Should be referred to a pediatrician for a full check up','Unilateral cases have a better prognosis.','We can visualize the fundus in dense cataract with an indirect ophthalmoscope.','Urgent treatment is needed in blue dot cataract.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**The book prints option A as an instruction, in the same words the question uses.** `content\\ophtho\\book\\ch16-paediatric.txt`, *Paediatric cataract* (printed 242–312), last bullet of the congenital cataract treatment block: \"The child should be **referred to a paediatrician** to assess the possible **association of systemic diseases or metabolic defects**.\" The reason is on the same page: \"In general, about **one-third** of congenital cataracts are associated with **systemic (genetic or metabolic) disease**.\" One infant in three with a congenital cataract has something else, and the eye is what brought them to a doctor.\n\n**Why each of the other three is false — and each is a separate teaching point:**\n\n**B. \"Unilateral cases have a better prognosis.\"** The opposite. A unilateral congenital cataract deprives one eye while its fellow develops normally, so the brain suppresses the deprived eye completely — dense unilateral deprivation amblyopia, the worst visual outcome in paediatric ophthalmology. The book records the sequel: \"**unilateral** congenital cataract can lead to **sensory squint**\", against \"**bilateral** congenital cataract can lead to **nystagmus** with poor visual prognosis\". Both are bad; the unilateral case is the one where the amblyopia is deepest and hardest to reverse, because there is a good eye competing. `L18) Cataract II` gives paediatric cataract the key finding \"VA loss + **amblyopia risk**\", and this bank keys amblyopia as the commonest complication at `opqb-t9-393`.\n\n**C. \"We can visualize the fundus in dense cataract with an indirect ophthalmoscope.\"** No instrument sees through a dense cataract — that is what *dense* means. The indirect ophthalmoscope gives a wider field and penetrates hazy media **better** than the direct, which is why it is the paediatric instrument, but better is not through. `L18`'s pre-operative rule is unambiguous: \"**B-scan ultrasound if fundus not visible** → rule out retinal detachment / tumour\", and \"If not visible → **B-scan ultrasound mandatory**\". This bank keys ultrasound for exactly that at `opqb-t9-371` and `opqb-t9-376`.\n\n**D. \"Urgent treatment is needed in blue dot cataract.\"** Blue dot cataract is the book's own example of the opposite. Its two-column table divides paediatric lens opacities into **not visually significant** — \"do not require treatment (**e.g., blue dot cataract, sutural cataract**)\" — and **visually significant** — \"dense and large enough to cause visual impairment **e.g., lamellar cataract**\". `L17) Cataract I` lists blue dot among the congenital morphologies (\"lamellar, **blue dot**, anterior pyramidal, posterior polar, membranous, sutural\") without giving it any urgency. Urgency belongs to the dense central opacity: \"Visually significant cataract requires **immediate treatment** once diagnosed to avoid **amblyopia**\", and `L18` lists \"**Dense congenital cataract** — prevent amblyopia (**within weeks**)\" among the urgent indications. This bank keys the same distinction from the other side at `opqb-t9-384`.\n\n**The one-line version:** in an infant with a cataract, the eye is urgent only if the opacity is visually significant — but the **child** always needs a paediatrician.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A third of congenital cataracts carry systemic or metabolic disease, so every infant with one is referred to a paediatrician; unilateral cases do worse, dense lenses hide the fundus, and blue dot cataract needs no treatment.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.55 (Q364), key p.62' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-365', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'Regarding the **postoperative complications of cataract surgery**:',
    options:['The most common is opacification of posterior capsule.','The least serious is endothelial trauma.','Endophthalmitis is easily treated with systemic antibiotics.','Astigmatism is more common after phacoemulsification than after extra-capsular extraction.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**`L18) Cataract II` prints the ranking twice, and option A is it.** Postoperative complications panel: \"**Posterior Capsule Opacification (PCO)** — Months–years. **Most common late complication.** Lens epithelial cells migrate on the posterior capsule → 'after-cataract'. Presents as gradual return of visual blurring. Tx: **Nd:YAG laser capsulotomy**.\" Its revision page: \"Q: **Most common post-op complication?** A: **PCO** — treated by Nd:YAG laser capsulotomy.\"\n\n**Why PCO is inevitable rather than unlucky.** Phaco deliberately leaves the posterior capsule in place, because the capsular bag is what holds the IOL centred (`opqb-t9-354`). Lens epithelial cells survive on the residual anterior capsule and equator; over months they migrate across the back of the bag and turn it into a translucent membrane. `L18` notes that a foldable acrylic optic gives \"**Reduced PCO vs PMMA**\", so material choice slows it, but nothing prevents it. Its clinical picture is asked as a vignette at `opqb-t9-380` and its treatment at `opqb-t9-349`.\n\n**Why the other three are false:**\n\n| Option | Why it is wrong |\n|---|---|\n| **The least serious is endothelial trauma.** | Nearly the reverse. The corneal endothelium **cannot regenerate**, so cells lost to phaco energy or instrument contact are lost permanently; enough loss and the cornea decompensates. `L18`: \"**Corneal Decompensation** — Bullous keratopathy from **endothelial cell loss** — especially in Fuchs' dystrophy patients … Tx: **penetrating or endothelial keratoplasty (DSAEK/DMEK)**.\" A complication whose treatment is a corneal graft is not the least serious of anything. |\n| **Endophthalmitis is easily treated with systemic antibiotics.** | Wrong drug, wrong route, wrong adverb. `L18`: \"Tx: **urgent vitreous tap + intravitreal antibiotics (vancomycin + ceftazidime)**. Severe: **vitrectomy**.\" The vitreous is avascular, so systemic antibiotics reach it poorly; the drug is injected into the eye. And it is \"the **most serious** vision-threatening complication\", not an easy one — this bank keys that at `opqb-t9-391` and builds the vignette at `opqb-t9-355`. |\n| **Astigmatism is more common after phacoemulsification than after extra-capsular extraction.** | Exactly inverted. `L18` gives phaco a \"**Self-sealing incision — NO sutures needed → less astigmatism**\" through a 2.2–3.0 mm wound, and ECCE an 8–10 mm limbal incision that \"Requires 10/0 nylon wound sutures → **significant astigmatism**\". This bank keys the correct direction at `opqb-t9-381`. |\n\n**Read the four options as a ranking exercise**: A is about frequency, B and C about severity, D about a comparison between techniques. Three of them get the direction of the comparison backwards, which is the commonest way this bank builds a \"regarding\" question.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'PCO is the commonest complication of cataract surgery; endothelial loss is permanent, endophthalmitis needs intravitreal not systemic antibiotics, and phaco induces LESS astigmatism than ECCE.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.55 (Q365), key p.62' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-366', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'Regarding the **optical properties of the lens**:',
    options:['The radius of curvature of the anterior surface is 6 mm.','The refractive index of the cortex is 1.42.','The refractive index of the nucleus 1.38.','The dioptric power outside the eye is 70'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**⚠️ THE PRINTED KEY IS D, AND OPTION C IS WITHIN 0.006 OF THE FIGURE `L18) Cataract II` PRINTS. The key is recorded as the bank prints it; the arithmetic is set out below and not disputed.**\n\n**What the deck prints.** `L18) Cataract II`, *Anatomy of the Crystalline Lens*: \"Shape: **Biconvex — steeper posterior curve** · Structure: **Avascular, transparent** — nutrition via aqueous · Suspension: **Zonules from ciliary processes → equatorial capsule** · **Optical power: ~18–20 D (one-third of total ~60 D)** · **Refractive index: 1.386 (nucleus)** — increases with age.\" `L17) Cataract I` gives the dimensions — \"Equatorial diameter **~ 9 mm**, anteroposterior diameter **~ 4 mm**\" — and the physiology: \"Responsible for **⅓ of the refractive power** of the eye\".\n\n**Option by option against that:**\n\n| Option | Measured against the deck and standard optics |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Radius of the anterior surface is 6 mm.** | **Wrong, and inverted.** The standard unaccommodated values are ~**10 mm** anteriorly and ~**6 mm** posteriorly — which is exactly what `L18`'s \"**steeper posterior curve**\" means, since the smaller radius is the steeper surface. The option has taken the posterior figure and put it on the anterior surface. |\n| **B. Refractive index of the cortex is 1.42.** | **Wrong, and also inverted.** The lens is a gradient-index structure: the cortex is the *less* dense layer (~1.386) and the nucleus the denser (~1.40–1.42). Putting 1.42 on the cortex reverses the gradient, and reversing it is precisely what would abolish the myopic shift of nuclear sclerosis. |\n| **C. Refractive index of the nucleus 1.38.** | **This is the option that matches the deck.** `L18` prints \"Refractive index: **1.386 (nucleus)**\" — a difference of 0.006 from the option, i.e. the option is `L18`'s own figure rounded to two decimals. On standard optical values the nucleus is nearer 1.40–1.42 and 1.386 is the cortex figure, so `L18` itself uses the number in a way most texts do not; either way, the option agrees with the module's own deck. |\n| **D. The dioptric power outside the eye is 70** | **The printed key.** The point it is making is real and is the more examinable one: the lens contributes only ~18–20 D **inside** the eye because it is surrounded by aqueous and vitreous of refractive index ~1.336, so the index *step* at each surface is small. Take the same lens out into **air** and the step becomes ~0.386–0.42, and its power rises several-fold. **The specific number 70 is printed in neither deck** and is not derivable from the figures they give (the deck's own values, with radii of 10 mm and 6 mm, give well over 100 D in air), so it is the bank's figure — *not taken from the course material* — and it should be learned as \"far higher out of the eye than in it\" rather than as a number. |\n\n**The examinable fact underneath the whole question** is the one both decks print and neither option states cleanly: **the lens supplies about one third of the eye's ~60 D of refracting power, roughly 18–20 D, and the cornea supplies most of the rest.** The lens is a weak lens in a strong eye precisely because it sits in fluid.\n\n*(The 10 mm/6 mm radii, the gradient-index description and the in-air calculation are **not taken from the course material**; `L18` prints only the index of the nucleus, the power in situ and the words \"steeper posterior curve\".)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The lens gives ~18–20 D in situ — one third of the eye’s ~60 D — because it sits in fluid; its power in air is far higher, and its index rises from cortex to nucleus.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.55 (Q366), key p.62 — option C is within 0.006 of L18:71; see the explanation' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-367', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'In managing **unilateral aphakia** with **good vision in the second eye**:',
    options:['Anisokonia is great with contact lenses.','Glasses made of plastic are tolerated.','LASIK can be used.','Anterior chamber IOL after intracapsular cataract extraction is indicated.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**An intracapsular extraction leaves no capsule, and an IOL has to be supported by something. `L18) Cataract II` names the answer twice.** Under IOL position: \"**Anterior Chamber IOL (ACIOL)** — **When no capsular support.** Angle-supported or iris-fixated (Artisan® lens)\"; and under ICCE: \"**No capsular bag → IOL placed in anterior chamber (ACIOL)**\". Aphakia in one eye with a good fellow eye is the situation that most demands an intraocular correction, because it is the only correction that leaves the two retinal images close enough in size to be fused — so the ACIOL is not merely permissible here, it is indicated.\n\n**The magnification table `L17) Cataract I` prints is the whole logic of this question:** glasses ~**30 %**, contact lens ~**7–10 %**, IOL ~**3 %**. Only the last is genuinely comfortable for a monocular aphake, and `L17` calls the IOL \"**Most physiological**\".\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **Anisokonia is great with contact lenses.** | The reverse of what `L17` prints: for monocular aphakia a contact lens is \"**suitable** (image magnification **~ 7 – 10 %** → **minimal anisokonia** → no binocular diplopia)\". *Great* aniseikonia is the property of **spectacles** (~30 %), not contact lenses. The option has swapped the two rows of the deck's own table. |\n| **Glasses made of plastic are tolerated.** | The material is irrelevant to the optics. A plastic (CR-39 or polycarbonate) aphakic lens is lighter and thinner than glass and is more *comfortable to wear*, but it has the same +10 to +12 D power, the same vertex distance and therefore the same ~30 % magnification and the same **ring scotoma** (`L18`: \"Aphakic spectacles (+10–12 D: **thick, minifying, ring scotoma**)\"). What defeats spectacles in unilateral aphakia is aniseikonia, and no material changes it. See `opqb-t9-350`. |\n| **LASIK can be used.** | LASIK reshapes the **cornea**, and its range is a few dioptres of myopia or hyperopia — `L15,16) Cornea` teaches it as a refractive-error procedure. An aphakic eye needs **+10 to +12 D**, far beyond any corneal ablation, and ablating that much tissue would leave a cornea too thin and too aberrated to see through. This bank offers the same wrong option at `opqb-t9-401`, where the answer is again a secondary IOL. |\n\n**Note the ranking the topic builds across three questions:** `opqb-t9-350` says spectacles are the one thing that will not work; this question says an ACIOL is indicated when there is no capsule; `opqb-t9-401` says that when a contact lens becomes intolerable the answer is a **secondary** IOL. All three are the same rule — get the correction as close to the nodal point as the eye's remaining anatomy allows.\n\n*(That plastic aphakic lenses are lighter but optically identical, and the dioptric ceiling of LASIK, are **not taken from the course material**.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'After ICCE there is no capsular support, so an anterior chamber IOL is the indicated correction for unilateral aphakia; spectacles, plastic or not, remain unfusable and LASIK cannot reach +10 D.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.55 (Q367), key p.62' }

,

  { id:'opqb-t9-368', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'The photograph shows one eye in ordinary illumination. **The iris is brown and the pupil is undilated. The whole pupillary aperture, edge to edge, is filled by a dense uniform white-grey material carrying a few faint pale striae; two small rectangular flash reflexes sit on its surface. Nothing of the fundus and no darker central zone can be made out through it.** What is the effect of the shown photo on vision?',
    options:['VA less than CF','VA more than CF','No or mild impairment','Variable effect'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"*(⚠️ THIS QUESTION HAS A FIGURE, and the bank's printed stem is only \"What is the effect of the shown photo on vision?\" — it names no finding at all, so it cannot be answered without the photograph. The stem above restates **what is visible** in the printed image so that the entry stands alone. The crop does not exist yet; its column, page and pixel bounds are recorded in `gg-t9.array.js`. Nothing in the restatement names the stage of the cataract, because doing so would answer the question. Q368, `opqb-t9-369` and `opqb-t9-370` are a graded set printed on the same page, and `opqb-t9-371` reuses this photograph.)*\n\n**A lens through which nothing at all can be seen is one through which nothing at all is seen.** `L18) Cataract II` grades maturity by exactly the two things the picture shows — the appearance of the pupil and the red reflex:\n\n| Stage | VA, as `L18` prints it | Red reflex |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Incipient** | 6/18 – 6/6 (mild or none) | Clear centrally, dark periphery |\n| **Immature** | 6/18 – CF (variable) | Dim red reflex |\n| **Mature** | **HM only (not less!)** | **ABSENT** — \"Totally opaque white lens\" |\n| **Hypermature** | HM or less | Variable |\n\n**Hand movements is worse than counting fingers**, so a totally opaque white lens gives an acuity *less than* CF — which is option A. `L17) Cataract I` states the same range from the other side: \"Incipient, immature cortical cataract → 6/6 → HM · **Mature cataract → HM** · **Never PL or No PL due to cataract or any media opacity**.\"\n\n**That last clause is the most useful sentence in the whole topic.** `L18` repeats it as a boxed principle: \"Cataract alone (without posterior segment disease) should **NEVER** reduce vision to less than Hand Motion (HM). **VA < HM → suspect concurrent retinal or optic nerve pathology.**\" So the correct reading of this photograph is *less than CF, and specifically HM* — not \"as bad as you like\". An eye like this one seeing only perception of light has a second disease, which is exactly the situation this bank builds at `opqb-t9-376`.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What the picture would have had to show |\n|---|---|\n| **VA more than CF** | A partly opacified lens with some red reflex still coming back — the immature picture of `opqb-t9-369`. |\n| **No or mild impairment** | Peripheral opacities clear of the visual axis, with a clear centre — the incipient picture of `opqb-t9-370`. |\n| **Variable effect** | True of a **posterior subcapsular** cataract, whose effect swings with pupil size and illumination, and of hypermature lenses. Not true of a lens that is uniformly opaque: there is nothing left for the pupil to vary. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A totally opaque white lens filling the pupil with no red reflex is a mature cataract — vision falls to hand movements, worse than counting fingers, but never below it from cataract alone.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.56 (Q368, figure), key p.63' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-369', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'The photograph shows a widely dilated pupil filling most of the frame, with pigmented iris tissue framing it above and to the left. **The lens behind it is dark greenish-grey and largely translucent, with a wispy pale streak running across its upper half; the pupillary area is not uniformly white and light is still returning from behind it.** What is the effect of the shown photo on vision?',
    options:['VA less than CF','VA not less than CF','No or mild impairment','Variable effect'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"*(⚠️ THIS QUESTION HAS A FIGURE and the printed stem names no finding — see the note on `opqb-t9-368`, which is the first of the same graded set. The restatement above describes only what is visible. ⚠️ **Option B here is worded differently from its neighbours**: Q368 and `opqb-t9-370` print \"VA **more** than CF\", this one prints \"VA **not less** than CF\". The crop bounds are in `gg-t9.array.js`.)*\n\n**A lens that still lets light through is not yet mature, and a cataract that is not yet mature does not take vision below counting fingers.** `L18) Cataract II` grades the middle stage precisely: \"**Immature** — VA: **6/18 – CF (variable)** · Red Reflex: **Dim red reflex** · Partial opacification; **VA reduced but ≥ CF.** Water clefts visible. **Shadow test: POSITIVE** (iris shadow on lens).\" *Reduced but ≥ CF* is what option B says.\n\n**The finding that settles maturity at the bedside is the shadow test**, and `L18` prints the whole method: \"Shine a torch **obliquely from temporal side**. If an **iris shadow** appears on the lens (crescentic shadow) → **IMMATURE** cataract (partial opacity, light bends around opaque cortex). **NO shadow → MATURE** cataract (totally opaque, all light reflected).\" Its revision page asks it as a one-liner: \"Q: Shadow test negative =? A: **MATURE** cataract (totally opaque, no shadow cast).\" The picture here is the immature end of that: clear cortex remains anteriorly, so an oblique beam can still cast a crescent.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why the picture excludes it |\n|---|---|\n| **VA less than CF** | That is the mature lens of `opqb-t9-368` — uniformly white, no red reflex, HM only. Here light is still coming back. |\n| **No or mild impairment** | Incipient disease, where the opacities sit in the periphery and the centre is clear (`L18`: \"Clear centrally, dark periphery\", VA \"6/18 – 6/6\"). The opacity here already crosses the pupil. |\n| **Variable effect** | The answer for a lens whose effect depends on pupil size — a posterior subcapsular plaque, worst in miosis and bright light. Nothing in an immature cortical opacity behaves that way once it has reached the axis. |\n\n**The three photographs on this page are a maturity ladder**, and reading them is the skill being tested: uniformly white → less than CF; partly opaque → not less than CF; peripheral only → no or mild impairment.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'An immature cataract is partly opacified with a dim red reflex and a positive shadow test; vision is reduced but never below counting fingers.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.56 (Q369, figure), key p.63' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-370', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'The photograph is a dark-field slit-lamp view. **A broad bright slit beam falls obliquely on the lens, with a large specular highlight in the middle of it. To the right of the beam, against an otherwise dark lens, a fan of fine pale lines runs inward from the periphery; the central part of the lens under the beam is clear.** What is the effect of the shown photo on vision?',
    options:['VA less than CF','VA more than CF','No or mild impairment','Variable effect'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"*(⚠️ THIS QUESTION HAS A FIGURE and the printed stem names no finding — see `opqb-t9-368` for the note on this graded set. The restatement above describes only what is visible; the crop bounds are in `gg-t9.array.js`.)*\n\n**Opacities that spare the visual axis spare the vision.** `L18) Cataract II` grades the earliest stage: \"**Incipient** — VA: **6/18 – 6/6 (mild or none)** · Red Reflex: **Clear centrally, dark periphery** · **Spoke-like cortical opacities at periphery; minimal visual complaint.**\" `L17) Cataract I` calls the same stage \"Cortical (stages) — **Incipient → water vacuoles**\".\n\n**Why the position matters more than the amount.** `L18`'s cortical panel states the rule outright: \"**Impact on VA depends on proximity to visual axis**\", and adds \"**Glare** prominent when opacities are **equatorial**\". A patient with an armful of peripheral cortical spokes can read 6/6 on a chart in a dim room and still complain bitterly about headlights, because the spokes scatter light without blocking the axis. That is why the deck marks cortical cataract's most-troubled-by column as \"Any visual task (**variable**)\" while its VA column reads \"Varies (**axis prox.**)\".\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it would need |\n|---|---|\n| **VA less than CF** | A totally opaque lens — `opqb-t9-368`. There is a clear central zone here. |\n| **VA more than CF** | True but far too weak: an incipient cataract is not merely better than counting fingers, it is often 6/6. The question offers a better-fitting option, and on a \"best answer\" paper the more precise one wins. |\n| **Variable effect** | The posterior subcapsular answer. A PSC sits at the nodal point on the posterior capsule and its effect swings with pupil size — worst in miosis, bright light and near work (`L18`: \"Worst symptoms: near vision, bright light, **miosis**\"). Peripheral cortical spokes have no such swing. |\n\n**Note the deliberate contrast across the page:** the same four option letters serve three different pictures, and only the picture distinguishes them. Whoever cuts these crops must keep them separate — `opqb-t9-371` reuses the Q368 photograph, which is the trap.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Incipient cortical spokes sit in the periphery, leave the axis clear and cause no or mild impairment — the effect of a cortical opacity depends on how close it is to the visual axis.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.56 (Q370, figure), key p.63' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-371', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'How to check for **retinal detachment** in patients with **mature cataract** as in the photo?',
    options:['Direct ophthalmoscope','Indirect ophthalmoscope','US','All of the above'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"*(This question carries a figure — the same clinical photograph used at `opqb-t9-368`, a pupil filled edge to edge by a dense white opacity — but unlike the other three on the page it **is answerable without it**, because the stem itself says \"in patients with mature cataract\". The picture only illustrates the obstruction. Crop bounds are in `gg-t9.array.js`.)*\n\n**No optical instrument sees through an opaque lens; sound does not need to.** `L18) Cataract II` makes it a rule of the pre-operative work-up: \"**B-scan ultrasound if fundus not visible → rule out retinal detachment / tumour**\", and repeats it in its clinical-approach slide: \"**If not visible → B-scan ultrasound mandatory.**\" Ultrasound works because the eye is mostly water: the probe sends sound through the lids and the sound is reflected by acoustic interfaces, so a detached retina appears as a bright membrane tethered at the disc no matter how white the lens is.\n\n**Why an optical instrument cannot substitute:**\n\n| Option | Why it fails here |\n|---|---|\n| **Direct ophthalmoscope** | Needs an almost clear media; it gives a high-magnification, very narrow field and is the first thing a cataract defeats. This bank uses that fact as a diagnostic clue at `opqb-t9-396`, where \"difficulty in seeing fundus details\" through a clear cornea points at the lens. |\n| **Indirect ophthalmoscope** | Better than the direct through hazy media, and the right instrument for detachment **when you can see** — wide field, stereoscopic, reaches the periphery. But *better through haze* is not *through opacity*: a mature lens transmits no image. This bank keys the same misconception as false at `opqb-t9-364`, option C. |\n| **All of the above** | Cannot be right once two of the three do not work. |\n\n**Why the question is asked about detachment specifically.** Finding a detachment before surgery changes everything: it explains why the vision is worse than the cataract accounts for, it changes the operation (a combined procedure rather than a phaco), and above all it changes what the patient is told about the outcome. `L18` lists the pre-operative posterior segment tests as a set — B-scan, ERG \"if macular disease suspected\", \"OCT when possible in hypermature cataract\" — and this bank asks each of them separately at `opqb-t9-376`, `opqb-t9-377` and `opqb-t9-385`.\n\n*(The physical reason ultrasound penetrates an opaque lens — acoustic rather than optical transmission — is **not taken from the course material**; the deck states the indication without the physics.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A mature cataract blocks every optical route to the fundus, so B-scan ultrasound is mandatory to exclude retinal detachment before surgery.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.56 (Q371, figure), key p.63' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-372', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'The photograph is a retro-illumination view: **an orange-red glow fills a round field, with a bright white round reflex at the upper left and a smaller white dot below it. Biting inward from the right edge of the field is an irregular dark opacity with jagged finger-like projections that reach part-way towards the centre; the middle of the field is otherwise clear.** What is the type of cataract in this photo?',
    options:['Incipient','Mature','Cortical','Immature'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"*(⚠️ THIS QUESTION HAS A FIGURE, and the bank's printed stem is only \"What is the type of cataract in this photo?\" — it cannot be answered without the picture. The stem above restates **what is visible** and stops short of naming the type. The crop bounds and column are in `gg-t9.array.js`. `opqb-t9-373` on the same page prints an almost identical stem with a different photograph and one different option.)*\n\n**Retro-illumination puts the light behind the lens**, so any opacity is seen as a **dark** shape silhouetted against the red reflex rather than as a white one. `L18) Cataract II` describes each morphology by exactly that appearance:\n\n| Morphology | Red reflex appearance, as `L18` prints it |\n|---|---|\n| **Nuclear** | \"Central distortion / **dark nucleus** against reflex\" — a *central* darkening |\n| **Cortical** | \"Begins as **wedge-shaped / spoke-like peripheral opacities**\" · \"**Appear dark against the red reflex**\" · \"**Dark spokes peripherally**\" |\n| **PSC** | \"**Dark plaque at posterior pole**\" — a discrete central plaque, granular |\n\n**The picture is the middle row.** The opacity is at the **edge** of the field, wedge-shaped, with projections running inward — the deck's \"spoke-like peripheral opacities\" — and the centre of the field is still transmitting red. `L18` adds the two clinical corollaries: \"**Impact on VA depends on proximity to visual axis**\" and \"Cortical spokes are often **asymmetric and can be missed without dilation**\", which is why this photograph was taken through a dilated pupil.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong, and note that only ONE of them is even the same kind of answer:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Incipient** | A **maturity** stage, not a morphology — and although incipient cortical cataract does look like this, \"Incipient\" describes *how far along* the lens is, while the question's other options mix the two axes. Where a maturity answer and a morphology answer both fit, the morphology is what a retro-illumination photograph shows; and the field here is too extensively involved for the deck's incipient description (\"minimal visual complaint\"). |\n| **Mature** | \"Totally opaque white lens\", \"Red Reflex: **ABSENT**\". A mature lens returns no red glow at all, so there could be no red field to silhouette anything against. |\n| **Immature** | \"**Dim** red reflex\", partial opacification. Possible as a stage, but again a maturity term rather than the morphology the picture displays. |\n\n**⚠️ The two axes this bank keeps mixing.** `L17) Cataract I` classifies cataract three ways — chronological, aetiological and **morphological** — and its morphological list runs \"Nuclear · Cortical (stages: incipient, immature, mature, hypermature)\". So *cortical* is the morphology and *incipient/immature/mature/hypermature* are stages **within** it. An option list containing three stages and one morphology is asking which axis the photograph reveals.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Wedge-shaped opacities silhouetted dark against the red reflex at the periphery of a dilated pupil are cortical spokes.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.57 (Q372, figure), key p.63' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-373', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'The photograph is a slit-lamp view of an eye with a brown iris and an undilated pupil. **A broad band of light crosses the lens; within the pupil the deepest, most central layers of the lens stand out as a denser pale band, while the more superficial layers on either side of it stay dark. A bright specular highlight sits on the lens surface to the right.** What is the type of cataract in this photo?',
    options:['Incipient','Mature','Nuclear','Immature'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"*(⚠️ THIS QUESTION HAS A FIGURE and the printed stem is only \"What is the type of cataract in this photo?\" — see the note on `opqb-t9-372`, its twin on the same page. The restatement above describes only what the optical section shows. ⚠️ **Option C differs between the two questions**: \"Cortical\" at Q372, \"**Nuclear**\" here. Crop bounds in `gg-t9.array.js`.)*\n\n**A slit-lamp optical section cuts the lens open, and where the density lies is the diagnosis.** `L17) Cataract I` names the layers first: \"Consistency — Young age → fluid · Older age → **center solid ('nucleus')**, surrounding periphery fluid ('**cortex**')\". `L18) Cataract II` then gives each morphology its slit-lamp appearance:\n\n| Morphology | Slit-lamp, as `L18` prints it | Location |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Nuclear** | \"**Yellow/brown nucleus**\" | \"**Central nucleus**\" |\n| **Cortical** | \"Spoke-like **peripheral** opacities\" | \"Peripheral cortex\" |\n| **PSC** | \"Granular **posterior capsule** plaque\" | \"Posterior capsule\" |\n\n**The picture shows the density in the middle of the section, with the outer layers still clear** — the central pattern, not the peripheral one and not a plaque on the back surface. `L17` gives the same entity its colour progression: \"Nuclear → **grey, yellow brown, black**.\"\n\n**Everything else worth knowing about this lens follows from where the opacity sits.** The nucleus is the eye's optical centre, so a nuclear cataract raises the refractive index on the visual axis: \"**Increased nuclear refractive index → acquired myopia**\", \"Interferes predominantly with **distant** vision\", \"'**Second sight**': elderly regain near vision temporarily\" (`L18`). That is the family of questions this topic keeps returning to — `opqb-t9-360`, `opqb-t9-362`, `opqb-t9-398`, `opqb-t9-402`, `opqb-t9-407`, and the existing `opqb-t1-70`.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Incipient** | A maturity stage, and specifically the *cortical* sequence's first stage (`L17`: \"Cortical (stages) — Incipient → water vacuoles\"). It describes peripheral vacuoles, not a dense centre. |\n| **Mature** | \"Totally opaque **white** lens\", red reflex absent, whole cortex and nucleus opacified (`L17`: \"Mature → **complete opacification of lens cortex & nucleus**\"). Here the outer layers are still clear enough to be dark against the beam rather than white. |\n| **Immature** | \"**Incomplete** opacification of lens cortex & nucleus\" (`L17`) with a dim red reflex — a stage term again, and one that says nothing about which layer is involved. |\n\n**The same warning as its twin:** three of the four options are maturity stages and one is a morphology. A slit-lamp optical section is a picture of *where*, so the morphology is what it can answer.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A dense central band in the optical section with clear surrounding layers is a nuclear cataract — the morphology defined by opacity of the lens nucleus.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.57 (Q373, figure), key p.63' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-374', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'A **34-year-old man** has **early cataract in both eyes**. O/E, the density of cataract was **exactly the same in both eyes**; with glasses, the **right eye cannot be corrected to better than 6/60** and **appears deviated inwards by 25degree**. The **left eye was corrected to 6/12** and the patient **had not experienced diplopia**. Additional questioning reveals that the patient had tried **occlusion therapy of the right eye dating since childhood**. What is your advice about this condition?',
    options:['Perform cataract extraction for both eyes as soon as possible.','Wait until maturity then perform cataract extraction for both eyes.','Perform cataract extraction for the left eye and never for the right eye.','Perform cataract extraction for the left eye and inform the patient that the right eye will improve only to his pre-cataract level so surgery is not recommended.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Every clue in this vignette points away from the cataract and towards a right eye that has been amblyopic since childhood.**\n\n| Finding in the stem | What it establishes |\n|---|---|\n| Cataract density **exactly the same in both eyes** | The lens cannot explain a 6/60 against a 6/12. Equal cause, unequal effect → the difference is not in the lens. |\n| **Deviated inwards by 25°** | A large-angle esotropia — and `L18) Cataract II` gives paediatric cataract the special feature \"Leukocoria, **squint**\", while `content\\ophtho\\book\\ch16-paediatric.txt` lists **squint** as one of the mechanisms of amblyopia. |\n| **No diplopia** despite a 25° deviation | An adult who acquires a squint sees double. One who never sees double has **suppressed** that eye — a lifelong adaptation, not a recent event. |\n| **Occlusion therapy … since childhood** | Patching is the treatment of amblyopia. It tells you the diagnosis was made decades ago and the eye did not recover. |\n\n**So the right eye's 6/60 is its long-standing amblyopic acuity, and the early cataract on top of it is contributing very little.** Removing the lens gives back only what the lens was taking away — the eye will return to roughly what it saw before the cataract started, which is still 6/60 or thereabouts. `content\\ophtho\\book\\ch16-paediatric.txt` sets out why: amblyopia is a developmental failure of the visual pathway during the sensitive period — \"**Form deprivation and occlusion amblyopia** (opacities in ocular media such as cataract…)\" — and by 34 that window is long closed.\n\n**Why the left eye is nevertheless operated.** It is this man's only functioning eye, and it is already down to 6/12 from an early cataract that will go on. `L18` lists as an elective indication \"VA below driving standard (typically **<6/12** in driving eye)\" and \"Patient-reported **functional disability**\", and in a patient whose fellow eye sees 6/60 the threshold to operate is *lower*, not higher.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it is wrong |\n|---|---|\n| **Both eyes as soon as possible** | Operating the right eye subjects an amblyopic eye to the risks of surgery — `L18`'s complication list runs from endothelial loss to **endophthalmitis** — for a gain measured in nothing. |\n| **Wait until maturity, then both** | \"Wait for maturity\" is obsolete practice. Modern indications are functional, not morphological; and waiting lets an immature lens swell into **phacomorphic** glaucoma (`opqb-t9-404`) while the patient's only good eye stays at 6/12. |\n| **Left eye, and *never* for the right** | The right advice with the wrong word. *Never* is unjustifiable: a lens that later matures can obstruct the fundus view, swell, or leak (phacolytic glaucoma, `opqb-t9-405`), and any of those makes removal necessary regardless of visual potential. The keyed option is better precisely because it explains the **prognosis** to the patient instead of closing the door. |\n\n**The rule to carry away:** before promising a patient anything from cataract surgery, ask what the eye saw *before* the cataract. `opqb-t9-378` and `opqb-t9-409` are the same lesson in different clothing.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A dense amblyopia from childhood limits an eye to its pre-cataract acuity, so lens surgery on that eye is not recommended — the fellow eye is operated on functional grounds.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.57 (Q374), key p.63' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-375', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'**A-scan ultrasonography** of the eye is used to calculate the power of the **intraocular lens** by measuring',
    options:['Corneal curvature','Refractive index.','Depth of the anterior chamber.','Axial length of the eye.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**An A-scan is a one-dimensional ultrasound trace: it measures DISTANCES along a single axis, and the distance it is used for is the length of the eye.** `L18) Cataract II`, biometry: \"**Axial length** (IOL Master/Lenstar preferred) · **Keratometry** (corneal curvature) · Formulae: **SRK/T** (standard), Haigis, Holladay (long/short eyes) · Target refraction: plano (standard) or slight myopia in elderly.\" `L17) Cataract I` states the pair the same way: \"IOL → power (**biometry**) … Based on measurements of ocular dimensions and biometric characteristics (**corneal curvature, axial length**) and **effective lens position**.\"\n\n**Why axial length is the number the calculation is most sensitive to.** The IOL has to place the image on the retina, and the retina's position is the axial length. An error of 1 mm in axial length produces roughly 2.5–3 D of refractive error at the spectacle plane — which is why the decks name the **IOL Master / Lenstar** (optical biometers) as preferred over contact ultrasound: pressing a probe on the cornea indents it and shortens the reading. This bank asks the same fact in reverse at `opqb-t9-382`, whose printed box reads \"IOL power depends largely **on axial length and corneal curvature**.\"\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What actually measures it, and where it fits |\n|---|---|\n| **Corneal curvature** | Genuinely part of the calculation — but it is measured by **keratometry**, an *optical* measurement of the size of a reflected image, not by ultrasound. This is the trap of the question: a true statement about biometry attached to the wrong instrument. `opqb-t8-317` in the cornea topic asks what keratometry measures. |\n| **Refractive index.** | Not measured in a patient at all. Formulae assume standard indices for cornea, aqueous and vitreous; there is no clinical way to measure an individual's. |\n| **Depth of the anterior chamber.** | An A-scan trace does show the anterior chamber depth, and modern formulae (Haigis, Holladay) use it to predict **effective lens position** — the term `L17` prints. But it is a refinement, not the measurement the A-scan exists for, and the classical SRK formula uses only axial length and keratometry. |\n\n**A-scan versus B-scan, since the topic uses both:** the **A**-scan is a single line of sight giving *amplitude against distance* — a ruler. The **B**-scan sweeps that line to build a two-dimensional picture — an image, used to look for retinal detachment or tumour behind an opaque lens (`opqb-t9-371`, `opqb-t9-376`). Same physics, different question.\n\n*(The 1 mm ≈ 2.5–3 D sensitivity and the indentation error of contact ultrasound are **not taken from the course material**; the decks name the measurements and the formulae without quantifying the error.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A-scan biometry measures axial length; keratometry supplies corneal curvature, and the two together drive the IOL power formula.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.57 (Q375), key p.63' }

,

  { id:'opqb-t9-376', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'A **66-year-old, insulin dependent diabetic** patient presents with **bilateral gradual deterioration of vision** of one-year duration. Ocular examination: vision in the **right eye is H.M.**, and in the **left eye is PL.**, and **bilateral mature cataracts**. **The fundus of both eyes is not seen. Before taking the decision of surgery, the following test is of value:**',
    options:['Fluorescein angiography.','Ultrasonography.','Visual field plotting.','Gonioscopy.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"Fundus is not visible due to mature cataract; **ultrasound helps evaluate retina and vitreous before surgery.**\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study. **This vignette is the antecedent of `opqb-t9-377`, `opqb-t9-378` and `opqb-t9-379`**, each of which opens \"In the previous case\" — all three refer back to *this* patient, not to the question immediately before them.)*\n\n**`L18) Cataract II` makes it a rule, not a preference:** \"**B-scan ultrasound if fundus not visible → rule out retinal detachment / tumour**\", and again in the clinical-approach slide, \"If not visible → **B-scan ultrasound mandatory**\". Ultrasound is the one modality that does not need the media to be clear, because it images by reflected sound rather than transmitted light.\n\n**Two features of this particular patient raise the stakes.** He is an **insulin-dependent diabetic**, so the eye behind that white lens may hold a tractional detachment, a vitreous haemorrhage or advanced proliferative disease — `L23) Ocular manifestations of systemic diseases` states that diabetes \"may have various ocular complications including refractive changes and cataract formation, but **the most important is retinopathy**\". And the **left eye sees only PL**, which by the decks' own principle is *worse than a cataract can account for*: `L18` prints \"Cataract alone (without posterior segment disease) should **NEVER** reduce vision to less than Hand Motion (HM). **VA < HM → suspect concurrent retinal or optic nerve pathology**\", and `L17) Cataract I` the same — \"**Never PL or No PL due to cataract** or any media opacity\". The right eye at HM is explicable by the cataract; the left eye at PL is not, and something in it must be found before it is opened.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it cannot be done, or would not help |\n|---|---|\n| **Fluorescein angiography.** | Photographs dye in the retinal circulation, and a camera cannot focus on a fundus it cannot see. Through a mature cataract there is no image to capture. It is the right investigation for this man's retinopathy **after** the lens is out. |\n| **Visual field plotting.** | Perimetry needs the patient to see a target. At HM and PL the only field information obtainable is **projection of light in four quadrants** — a bedside test, not a plotted field. `L18` lists exactly that: \"Projection of light in all 4 directions (dense cataract)\". This bank asks it at `opqb-t9-408`. |\n| **Gonioscopy.** | Examines the **drainage angle** with a mirrored contact lens. Useful in glaucoma; it says nothing about the retina, and nothing in this vignette suggests raised pressure. |\n\n**Where this sits in the pre-operative sequence:** B-scan first, because it can find a contraindication; then electrophysiology for retinal *function* if the anatomy looks intact (`opqb-t9-377`, `opqb-t9-385`); the whole set is `L18`'s \"Posterior Segment Evaluation\" panel.",
    objective:'When the fundus cannot be seen through a mature cataract, B-scan ultrasound is mandatory before surgery — and PL vision means a second, non-lenticular disease.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.58 (Q376), key p.63 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-377', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'The **66-year-old insulin-dependent diabetic** described above — **bilateral mature cataracts**, right eye **HM**, left eye **PL**, fundus not visible in either eye — is being assessed further. For **visual prognosis**, the following investigation is **obligatory**:',
    options:['Electrophysiological studies.','Fundus fluorescein angiography.','Computerized axial tomography.','Post-prandial blood glucose test.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"*(⚠️ **BACK-REFERENCE REPAIRED.** The bank prints only \"In the previous case the next step to for visual prognosis, the following investigation is obligatory:\" — the missing word and all — and the case referred to is **`opqb-t9-376`**, whose vignette is restated above so this entry stands alone. The restatement deliberately does **not** name the test keyed in `opqb-t9-376`.)*\n\n**Ultrasound shows you the retina's SHAPE; electrophysiology shows you whether it WORKS.** That is the distinction the question turns on, and `L18) Cataract II`'s pre-operative panel prints both, in that order: \"**B-scan ultrasound if fundus not visible** → rule out retinal detachment / tumour · **Electroretinogram (ERG) if macular disease suspected** · OCT when possible in hypermature cataract\", alongside \"**Potential acuity measurement (PAM) if doubt about macular function**\".\n\n**An attached retina is not necessarily a functioning one**, and in a long-standing insulin-dependent diabetic that gap is exactly where the risk lies: macular oedema, macular ischaemia and old exudative maculopathy can all leave a structurally normal-looking retina that will never give useful acuity. An ERG (and, for the macula specifically, a pattern or multifocal ERG, with a VEP for the pathway beyond) is the only way to interrogate function through an opaque lens, because it needs a flash of light and an electrode, not an image. That is what makes it **obligatory for prognosis** rather than merely useful — the word in the stem is *prognosis*, not diagnosis.\n\n**And it is the test that would have predicted this patient's outcome.** `opqb-t9-378` reveals that after surgery he reached only 6/36, because of a macular lesion that could not be diagnosed beforehand — which is precisely the lesion an ERG is done to look for. This bank keys the same investigation, in the same situation, at `opqb-t9-385` (\"mandatory before cataract surgery for a patient with hand motion visual acuity\").\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it is not the answer |\n|---|---|\n| **Fundus fluorescein angiography.** | Needs a clear media and a focusable fundus image. Through bilateral mature cataracts there is nothing to photograph. It becomes the right test for his retinopathy once the lenses are out. |\n| **Computerized axial tomography.** | Images bone and gross orbital anatomy. It cannot resolve retinal function, retinal attachment or macular structure, and nothing here suggests orbital or intracranial disease. `opqb-t9-390` keys CT as the investigation that is **not** required before cataract surgery. |\n| **Post-prandial blood glucose test.** | Important for the patient — glycaemic control governs progression of retinopathy, and `L23) Ocular manifestations of systemic diseases` devotes a slide to it (\"intensive glycemic control is associated with a **reduced risk** of newly diagnosed retinopathy\") — but it tells you nothing about **this eye's** visual potential, which is what \"visual prognosis\" asks. |\n\n*(That pattern/multifocal ERG addresses the macula while flash ERG addresses the whole retina, and that VEP tests the pathway beyond it, are **not taken from the course material**; `L18` prints \"Electroretinogram (ERG)\" alone.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Ultrasound answers whether the retina is attached; electrophysiology answers whether it works — and only the second gives a visual prognosis through an opaque lens.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.58 (Q377), key p.63 — back-reference to Q376 restated' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-378', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'In the **66-year-old insulin-dependent diabetic** described above — bilateral mature cataracts, right eye **HM**, left eye **PL**, fundus not visible before surgery — **after the extraction of his cataract the patient’s vision improved to 6/36 only**. The probable lesion seen in the fundus in this case, and which **could not be diagnosed prior to surgery**, would be:',
    options:['Macular affection by exudates.','Dense vitreous hemorrhage.','Massive traction retinal detachment.','Central retinal artery occlusion.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"Limited improvement **after surgery suggests pre-existing macular pathology.**\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. ⚠️ **BACK-REFERENCE REPAIRED:** the bank prints \"In the previous case After the extraction of his cataract **the above patient's** vision Improved to 6/36 only…\". \"The above patient\" is **`opqb-t9-376`**, not `opqb-t9-377`, which is itself a follow-on question and contains no patient. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**Read the number, then read the two words that constrain the answer.** The eye now sees **6/36** — poor, but form vision, with the media clear. And the lesion must be one that **could not have been diagnosed before surgery**, i.e. it must be invisible to the pre-operative work-up: invisible to B-scan (so it cannot be a detachment or a vitreous mass) and not obvious enough to have stopped the operation.\n\n**A diabetic macula thickened and studded with hard exudates fits both conditions exactly.** It is flat, so ultrasound sees nothing abnormal; it is a *retinal* lesion of a few hundred microns' thickness in the central few degrees, which is beneath the resolution of a B-scan. And it caps acuity at precisely this sort of level. `L23) Ocular manifestations of systemic diseases` lists \"**Hard exudates**\" among the signs of NPDR and gives diabetic maculopathy its own heading (\"Diffuse diabetic maculopathy\"), and `L18) Cataract II`'s clinical-approach slide asks the surgeon to check the \"**Macula: AMD, diabetic maculopathy, ERM**\" at the fundal examination — an examination this patient could not have.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong, and each is excluded by one of the two constraints:**\n\n| Option | Which constraint it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **Dense vitreous hemorrhage.** | *Would have been diagnosed.* A dense vitreous haemorrhage is one of the loudest findings on B-scan — a cavity full of mobile echoes. It also would not permit 6/36; the eye would still be seeing hand movements, because the blood is between the new IOL and the retina. |\n| **Massive traction retinal detachment.** | *Would have been diagnosed*, and would not allow 6/36. B-scan shows a tractional detachment as a tented membrane fixed to the disc — it is the specific thing `L18` sends you to ultrasound to \"rule out\". `opqb-t9-379` uses it as an option in the fellow eye for that reason. |\n| **Central retinal artery occlusion.** | Wrong tempo and wrong acuity. A CRAO is a **sudden**, painless, profound loss — count fingers or worse, with an afferent pupillary defect — not a chronic 6/36 discovered afterwards. It would also have shown a **RAPD** before surgery, and `L18` puts \"**Pupillary reflexes (relative afferent pupillary defect?)**\" in the pre-operative checklist precisely to catch that. |\n\n**The teaching point, stated plainly:** the pre-operative work-up can guarantee the retina is *attached*, never that it is *good*. That gap is why `opqb-t9-377` calls electrophysiology obligatory for prognosis, and why `opqb-t9-374` and `opqb-t9-409` are about telling the patient the truth before the operation rather than after.\n\n*(The resolution limit of B-scan against a flat macular lesion is **not taken from the course material**; the decks state the indication for ultrasound without its limits.)*",
    objective:'A flat diabetic maculopathy is invisible to pre-operative B-scan yet caps post-operative acuity — the one lesion that fits both "6/36 only" and "could not be diagnosed beforehand".',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.58 (Q378), key p.63 (explanation box printed) — back-reference to Q376 restated' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-379', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'In the **66-year-old insulin-dependent diabetic** described above — bilateral mature cataracts, right eye **HM**, **left eye PL**, fundus not visible — the probable **contraindication to surgery in the Lt. eye**, as discovered by **ultrasonography**, was probably:',
    options:['Massive tractional retinal detachment.','Optic neuritis.','Massive vitreous hemorrhage.','Macular exudates'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"*(⚠️ **BACK-REFERENCE REPAIRED:** the bank prints \"In the previous case The probable contraindication to surgery in the **Lt. eye** as discovered ultrasonography was probably:\" — the missing preposition is the source's. Only **`opqb-t9-376`** defines a left eye, so that is the antecedent. ⚠️ **The stem names ULTRASONOGRAPHY, which is `opqb-t9-376`'s own key — that leak is the bank's, printed on its own page, and is kept because it is printed.**)*\n\n**⚠️ THE PRINTED KEY IS C. The classical answer to \"contraindication found on B-scan\" is option A, and the divergence is set out here rather than disputed — `answer` always carries the bank's key.**\n\n**What the key asserts.** The left eye sees only **PL**, which both decks say a cataract alone cannot cause (`L17) Cataract I`: \"**Never PL or No PL due to cataract** or any media opacity\"; `L18) Cataract II`: \"VA < HM → **suspect concurrent retinal or optic nerve pathology**\"). So something else is in that eye, and in an insulin-dependent diabetic with proliferative disease a **massive vitreous haemorrhage** is a leading candidate — it is loud on B-scan, it is common in this exact patient, and removing the cataract would not restore any vision while blood still fills the cavity. Read that way, the key is saying: *do not do a lens operation on an eye whose problem is behind the lens.*\n\n**Why option A is the answer most textbooks would give.** A **massive tractional retinal detachment** is the finding `L18` sends the surgeon to B-scan for by name — \"B-scan ultrasound if fundus not visible → **rule out retinal detachment / tumour**\" — and it is the finding that most absolutely contraindicates a routine cataract extraction, because the eye has no visual potential to recover and because opening it invites further traction. It is also the option this bank itself uses as a distractor at `opqb-t9-378`.\n\n**How the two can be reconciled, if they can.** In modern practice neither is an absolute bar: a diabetic eye with vitreous haemorrhage or tractional detachment is usually managed by a **combined phaco + pars plana vitrectomy**, so \"contraindication\" means *contraindication to isolated cataract surgery*. On that reading both A and C are contraindications to doing the lens alone, and the bank has picked the one that better explains PL vision with no light localisation. The page prints a bare key letter with no explanation box, so nothing on it settles the matter.\n\n**Why the other two are wrong outright:**\n\n| Option | Why it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **Optic neuritis.** | Not an ultrasound diagnosis at all — B-scan cannot see optic nerve inflammation. It is also the wrong disease for a 66-year-old diabetic: optic neuritis is a young adult's demyelinating disease with **pain on eye movement** and a RAPD, and it recovers. |\n| **Macular exudates** | Flat, a few hundred microns thick, and beyond the resolution of B-scan — which is exactly why `opqb-t9-378` keys it as the lesion that **could not** be diagnosed before surgery. A lesion invisible to the test cannot be what the test discovered. |\n\n*(That combined phaco-vitrectomy is the modern management, and the resolution limit of B-scan, are **not taken from the course material**.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'PL vision behind a mature cataract means posterior segment disease; the bank keys massive vitreous haemorrhage as the ultrasound finding that contraindicates lens surgery alone.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.58 (Q379), key p.63 — back-reference to Q376 restated; key diverges from the classical answer, see the explanation' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-380', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'A patient who had a **successful cataract extraction with IOL implantation 5 years ago** now presents with **progressive deterioration in vision**. The examination reveals a **grayish membrane behind the IOL**. The treatment of choice is:',
    options:['The removal of the IOL.','Surgical exploration.','Yag Laser capsulotomy.','Medical treatment with topical steroids.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**\"A grayish membrane behind the IOL\" is a description of the posterior capsule, and a posterior capsule that has gone grey is posterior capsule opacification.** `L18) Cataract II`: \"**Posterior Capsule Opacification (PCO)** — **Months–years.** **Most common late complication.** **Lens epithelial cells migrate on the posterior capsule** → '**after-cataract**'. Presents as **gradual return of visual blurring**. Tx: **Nd:YAG laser capsulotomy** — outpatient, definitive, excellent outcomes.\" Every element of the vignette maps onto a phrase of that panel: five years is inside \"months–years\", \"progressive deterioration\" is \"gradual return of visual blurring\", and the membrane is behind the IOL because the IOL is in the bag and the capsule is the back wall of the bag.\n\n**Why the treatment is a laser and not an operation.** The capsule is a thin acellular membrane sitting a fraction of a millimetre behind the implant. An **Nd:YAG** laser is photodisruptive — it makes a mechanical opening by ionising a microscopic volume — so it can cut a central window in that membrane **without opening the eye**, in a clinic chair, in minutes, with the IOL left exactly where it is. `L18` calls it \"outpatient, definitive, excellent outcomes\"; `opqb-t9-349` asks which laser, and `opqb-t9-365` asks the frequency ranking.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it is wrong |\n|---|---|\n| **The removal of the IOL.** | The IOL is not the problem — it is clear, correctly placed and doing its job. Explanting an IOL from a five-year-old bag is difficult surgery with real endothelial and capsular risk, undertaken only for a decentred, dislocated or wrongly powered implant (`L18` lists \"IOL exchange\" only under **refractive surprise**). |\n| **Surgical exploration.** | Opens an eye to look for something already visible at the slit lamp. The diagnosis is made by looking; there is nothing to explore. |\n| **Medical treatment with topical steroids.** | PCO is a **proliferative and fibrotic** process — migrated lens epithelial cells laying down collagen — not an inflammatory one. Steroids do not reverse a fibrosed capsule, and prolonged topical steroid causes its own cataract and raises intraocular pressure (`opqb-t9-387`). |\n\n**One caution that keeps this question honest:** *gradual loss of vision in a pseudophakic eye is not automatically PCO.* The same patient could have developed diabetic maculopathy, AMD or cystoid macular oedema. What makes PCO the answer here is that the membrane was **seen** — the sign is in the stem, not inferred from the symptom.\n\n*(Which cells lay down the fibrosis, and the risks of IOL explantation, are **not taken from the course material**; `L18` names the cell type and the treatment without elaborating either.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A grey membrane behind the IOL years after surgery is PCO, treated definitively and non-invasively by Nd:YAG capsulotomy.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.58 (Q380), key p.63' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-381', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'The type of **cataract surgery** which results in the **least postoperative astigmatism**:',
    options:['Extra capsular cat. + IOL','Extra capsular cat with no IOL','Phacoemulsification + IOL','Intra capsular cataract extraction+IOL.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Post-operative astigmatism is a property of the WOUND, and the wound is the one thing these four operations differ in most.** `L18) Cataract II` prints the comparison directly:\n\n| Technique | Incision | Closure | Astigmatism |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Phacoemulsification** | \"**2.2 – 3.0 mm** (clear corneal or scleral tunnel)\" | \"**Self-sealing incision — NO sutures needed**\" | \"**→ less astigmatism**\" |\n| **ECCE** | \"**8 – 10 mm** limbal incision\" | \"Requires **10/0 nylon wound sutures**\" | \"**→ significant astigmatism**\" |\n| **ICCE** | \"**Large incision** — entire lens + capsule removed\" | — | Largest of all |\n\n**Why a big wound bends the cornea.** A limbal incision cuts across corneal lamellae; when it is pulled shut with sutures, the cornea flattens in the meridian of the suture and steepens 90° away from it — with-the-rule or against-the-rule astigmatism depending on where the wound was placed. The more of the circumference you cut and the tighter you tie it, the larger and the less predictable the effect, and it drifts for weeks as the sutures loosen. A 2.2 mm tunnel through clear cornea is small enough to be **self-sealing** — the pressure inside the eye holds its own valve shut — so nothing has to be tied and almost nothing is distorted. This is the same fact `opqb-t9-353` asks as \"earliest visual rehabilitation\" and `opqb-t9-365` offers inverted as a false statement.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why more astigmatism |\n|---|---|\n| **Extra capsular cat. + IOL** | An 8–10 mm sutured limbal wound, needed because a **rigid PMMA IOL** \"doesn't fold\" and must pass whole. `L18` says outright that it induces significant astigmatism. |\n| **Extra capsular cat with no IOL** | The same wound, so the same astigmatism, **plus** an aphakic eye needing +10 to +12 D of correction with all the problems that brings (`opqb-t9-350`). Strictly worse than the option above it. |\n| **Intra capsular cataract extraction+IOL.** | The largest wound of the four — the lens is delivered *with its capsule* — and the IOL has to go in the anterior chamber, `L18`'s ACIOL \"when no capsular support\", with its own complication list: \"bullous keratopathy, glaucoma, vitreous prolapse\". |\n\n**Where the astigmatism question goes next in practice:** if the patient has significant pre-existing corneal astigmatism, a **toric IOL** corrects it — `L18`: \"**Toric** — Corrects corneal astigmatism. **Axis must be marked pre-op.**\" Astigmatism after surgery is therefore something you both avoid (small wound) and can treat (toric optic).\n\n*(The mechanics of suture-induced flattening are **not taken from the course material**; the deck states the outcome without the mechanism.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Phaco with a foldable IOL through a 2.2–3.0 mm self-sealing wound induces the least astigmatism; ECCE and ICCE need large sutured incisions.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.58 (Q381), key p.63' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-382', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'The measurement of **intraocular lens power** prior to IOL implantation entails:',
    options:['Measuring the preoperative spectacle power.','Measuring the axial length of the eye.','Performing preoperative retinoscopy.','Careful fundus examination.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"IOL power depends largely **on axial length and corneal curvature.**\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. Note that it names **two** measurements while only one of them appears among the options — corneal curvature is offered as a distractor in the mirror-image question `opqb-t9-375`, where axial length is again the key. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**Biometry answers one question: where is the retina, and how strongly does the front of the eye already bend light?** `L18) Cataract II` prints the pair and the formulae: \"**Axial length** (IOL Master/Lenstar preferred) · **Keratometry** (corneal curvature) · Formulae: **SRK/T** (standard), Haigis, Holladay (long/short eyes) · Target refraction: **plano** (standard) or **slight myopia in elderly**.\" `L17) Cataract I` states the same inputs — \"measurements of ocular dimensions and biometric characteristics (**corneal curvature, axial length**) and **effective lens position**\".\n\n**Why the eye's own optics, not the patient's glasses, are what get measured.** The IOL replaces a lens of about **18–20 D** sitting inside a fluid-filled globe of a particular length. Whether the patient wore +3 or −6 in spectacles is a *consequence* of that geometry, not an input to it: a long eye and a short eye can wear the same glasses for different reasons, and after the natural lens is removed the old prescription describes an optical system that no longer exists.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it does not give IOL power |\n|---|---|\n| **Measuring the preoperative spectacle power.** | Describes the pre-operative eye with its own cataractous lens still in place — and in nuclear sclerosis that lens has been actively **changing** the refraction (`opqb-t9-360`, `opqb-t9-362`). Using it would build the cataract's myopic shift into the implant. |\n| **Performing preoperative retinoscopy.** | The same objection with a different instrument, and worse: retinoscopy through a dense cataract gives a dim, distorted or unobtainable reflex. It measures the whole eye's current refraction, not the two dimensions the formula needs. |\n| **Careful fundus examination.** | Essential before surgery for other reasons — `L18`'s checklist has \"Macula: AMD, diabetic maculopathy, ERM · Disc: glaucomatous cupping? · Periphery: retinal breaks\" — but it is a **prognostic** examination, not a measurement. It contributes nothing to the power calculation. |\n\n**What happens when the calculation is wrong:** `L18` gives it a name and a fix — \"**Refractive Surprise** — Unexpected post-op refraction error from **biometry inaccuracy**, wrong formula, or IOL misidentification. **Prevention: double-check calculations**, use modern formulae for outlier eyes. Tx: IOL exchange or piggyback IOL, LASIK enhancement.\"\n\n*(The reasoning about why spectacle power is not an input is **not taken from the course material**; the decks list the inputs without arguing against the alternatives.)*",
    objective:'IOL power is calculated from axial length and keratometry, not from the patient’s spectacle prescription or a pre-operative retinoscopy.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.58 (Q382), key p.63 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-383', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'A **morgagnian cataract** can cause one of the following complication:',
    options:['Retinal detachment.','Optic atrophy.','Acute rise of IOP.','Rubeosis iridis.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**A Morgagnian cataract is a hypermature lens whose cortex has liquefied, and liquefied cortex leaks. What leaks is protein, and protein blocks the drainage angle — an acute rise of intraocular pressure.**\n\n`L17) Cataract I` defines the lens: \"Cortical (stages) — **Hypermature** (shrinkage of lens capsule, liquefaction of cortex) — Typical (shrinkage of and calcification and cholesterol deposits on lens capsule) · **Morgagnian (liquefaction of cortex and sinking of nucleus)**.\" `L18) Cataract II` describes what it looks like: \"**MORGAGNIAN type: liquefied cortex, nucleus sinks inferiorly** (pathognomonic pseudohypopyon-like appearance). **Risk: phacolytic glaucoma.**\"\n\n**The mechanism, step by step, from `L17`:** \"**Degeneration of lens capsule → leakage of lens proteins →** immune reaction in anterior chamber (uveitis) → uveitic glaucoma\" and \"**clogging of TM by lens proteins macromolecules → secondary open angle glaucoma (trabecular)**\". `L18`'s summary table states it in one row: \"**Phacolytic Glaucoma · Hypermature/mature · Liquefied cortex leaks proteins · HM, pain, redness · Macrophages block TM · Urgent lens removal.**\" The presentation is acute and painful, which is why the option says *acute* rise of IOP.\n\n**This is the same fact asked three ways in this topic**: the treatment at `opqb-t9-351`, the lens it happens in at `opqb-t9-405`, and the complication here.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it does not follow from a Morgagnian lens |\n|---|---|\n| **Retinal detachment.** | Nothing about a liquefied cortex pulls on the retina. Detachment after cataract *surgery* is a recognised risk when the posterior capsule ruptures — `L18`: \"Posterior capsule rupture + vitreous loss (intraop) → higher risk of retinal detachment\" — but that is a surgical complication, not a complication of the cataract itself. |\n| **Optic atrophy.** | Would require sustained pressure or optic nerve disease. An untreated phacolytic glaucoma left long enough could damage the nerve, but that is a late consequence of the pressure, not the complication the lens causes; and it is not *acute*. |\n| **Rubeosis iridis.** | New iris vessels follow **retinal ischaemia** — proliferative diabetic retinopathy, central retinal vein occlusion, ocular ischaemic syndrome. A hypermature lens causes no ischaemia. (`opqb-t9-358` does key neovascular glaucoma for a *dislocated* lens, where the route is the retinal disturbance that accompanies dislocation, not the lens protein.) |\n\n**The clinical picture to recognise:** an elderly patient, long-blind in that eye, presents with a red painful eye, hand-movements vision, a very high pressure, and a white lens with a brown nucleus sitting low in it. The treatment is not a pressure operation but removal of the lens.\n\n*(That rubeosis requires ischaemia is **not taken from the cataract decks**; the module teaches it in `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders`.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A Morgagnian hypermature cataract leaks liquefied cortical protein into the anterior chamber, blocking the trabecular meshwork and causing acute phacolytic glaucoma.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.58 (Q383), key p.63' }

,

  { id:'opqb-t9-384', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'A **6-month infant** presenting with a **localized dot shaped anterior polar cataract** with **no other apparent ocular abnormality** is best managed by:',
    options:['Lensectomy with IOL.','Black pupil contact lens.','Extracapsular cataract extraction with IOL.','Follow up with visual acuity testing'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"*(Secondary chapter: `op-cat` — the decision rests on classifying the opacity, which is morphology; the action taken is a management one, so the entry is filed with the surgical chapter.)*\n\n**The book divides paediatric lens opacities into two boxes, and this one is in the box that is not treated.** `content\\ophtho\\book\\ch16-paediatric.txt`, *Paediatric cataract* (printed 242–312), prints a two-column table:\n\n| **not visually significant** | **visually significant** |\n|---|---|\n| \"**do not require treatment** (e.g., **blue dot cataract, sutural cataract**)\" | \"**dense and large enough to cause visual impairment** e.g., **lamellar cataract**\" |\n\n**A \"localized dot shaped anterior polar cataract\" is on the left of that line, and every word of the stem says so.** *Localized* and *dot shaped* mean small; *anterior polar* means it sits on the front surface of the lens, where the pupil can still see round it in dim light; and *no other apparent ocular abnormality* rules out the associated malformations that would change the plan. `L17) Cataract I` lists the same family of congenital morphologies — \"lamellar, blue dot, **anterior pyramidal**, posterior polar, membranous, sutural\" — without attaching urgency to any of the small ones.\n\n**So the management is to watch the vision, not the lens.** \"Follow up with visual acuity testing\" is exactly right for an infant, because what matters is not whether the dot enlarges but whether the eye is developing normally; a preferential-looking or fixation-behaviour assessment at intervals will show amblyopia developing long before the opacity looks different. The book's warning applies to the *other* box: \"**Visually significant cataract requires immediate treatment** once diagnosed to avoid **amblyopia**\", and `L18) Cataract II` lists \"**Dense congenital cataract** — prevent amblyopia (**within weeks**)\" among the **urgent** indications. This bank keys the mirror-image fact at `opqb-t9-364`, where \"urgent treatment is needed in blue dot cataract\" is offered as a false statement.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it is wrong |\n|---|---|\n| **Lensectomy with IOL.** | Removing a clear-enough lens from a 6-month-old converts an eye with good visual potential into an aphakic or pseudophakic one — an eye that cannot accommodate, that will need repeated refraction as it grows, and that carries the surgical risks for no visual gain. |\n| **Black pupil contact lens.** | An occluding lens is used to *stop* an eye seeing (for intractable diplopia, or cosmesis in a blind eye). Occluding an infant's eye is the fastest way to create the very amblyopia the follow-up exists to detect — the book names \"**occlusion amblyopia**\" as one of its mechanisms. |\n| **Extracapsular cataract extraction with IOL.** | The wrong operation as well as an unnecessary one. A child's lens has no hard nucleus, so it is aspirated, not expressed: `L17` prints \"**Children → irrigation / aspiration (soft lens)** · Adults → conventional ECCE, small incision cataract extraction\". |\n\n**And if surgery ever does become necessary in a child**, the aftercare is the harder half: the book's tutorial box reads \"**Visual rehabilitation after surgery:** Patients require treatment with **eyeglasses, bifocals, or contact lenses, & eye-patching**. Often, pediatric cataracts result in some degree of **amblyopia**\", and `L18` adds \"**Contact lens preferred < 2 years**; secondary IOL when eye growth stabilises (~2 years)\".\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A small localized anterior polar dot is not visually significant, so the infant is followed with visual acuity testing rather than operated on.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.59 (Q384), key p.63' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-385', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'The following test is **mandatory** before cataract surgery for a patient with **hand motion visual acuity**:',
    options:['Perimetry.','Electrophysiology','Genioscopy.','Fluorescein angiography.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Hand movements means the lens is mature, which means you cannot see the retina, which means you have to ask it a question it can answer in the dark.** `L18) Cataract II`'s pre-operative panel: \"**B-scan ultrasound** if fundus not visible → rule out retinal detachment / tumour · **Electroretinogram (ERG) if macular disease suspected** · OCT when possible in hypermature cataract\", with \"**Potential acuity measurement (PAM) if doubt about macular function**\" beside it.\n\n**Electrophysiology needs neither a clear media nor a cooperative acuity.** An ERG records the electrical response of the retina to a flash of light; a VEP records what arrives at the occipital cortex. Neither requires a focused image, so both work through a white lens — which is exactly why they are the tests that survive when everything optical has failed. `L17) Cataract I` fixes the acuity in question: \"Incipient, immature cortical cataract → 6/6 → HM · **Mature cataract → HM** · **Never PL or No PL due to cataract or any media opacity**.\"\n\n**Ultrasound and electrophysiology answer different questions and are not alternatives**: the B-scan says whether the retina is *attached*, the ERG says whether it *works*. This bank asks the first at `opqb-t9-376` and `opqb-t9-371`, and the second here and at `opqb-t9-377`. In the 66-year-old diabetic of `opqb-t9-378`, it is the second question that turned out to matter.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it cannot be done, or would not help |\n|---|---|\n| **Perimetry.** | A plotted field needs the patient to see and localise a target on a chart or bowl. At hand movements there is no acuity to run a perimeter with. What remains is the bedside substitute `L18` prints — \"**Projection of light in all 4 directions** (dense cataract)\" — which is a different test with a different name, and this bank asks it at `opqb-t9-408`. |\n| **Genioscopy.** | Printed for **gonioscopy**, examination of the drainage angle with a mirrored contact lens. It is a glaucoma test; it says nothing about retinal function and is not mandatory before a routine cataract operation. |\n| **Fluorescein angiography.** | Requires a fundus image to photograph. Through a mature cataract there is none. It is the test for this patient's retina **after** the lens comes out. |\n\n**Why \"mandatory\" is the right word.** The point of the test is not diagnosis but **consent**: a patient whose retina is electrically silent will not see after surgery, and must be told so before it, not after. That is the same lesson as `opqb-t9-374` and `opqb-t9-409`.\n\n*(The distinction between flash ERG, pattern ERG and VEP is **not taken from the course material**; `L18` prints \"Electroretinogram (ERG)\" alone.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'At hand-movements acuity the retina can only be interrogated electrically — ERG/VEP works through an opaque lens, where perimetry and angiography cannot.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.59 (Q385), key p.63' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-386', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'**Phacoemulsification** is the **preferred procedure** in the following condition:',
    options:['Cataract with a dislocated lens.','Black cataract.','Intumescent cataract.','Dense posterior subcapsular cateract.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Phaco needs zonules that hold and a nucleus the ultrasound can break. An intumescent cataract has both — and it needs the operation urgently.**\n\n`L17) Cataract I` defines the lens: \"**Intumescence** (lens fibres liquefaction and fluid imbibition → **swelling of lens**)\", and traces the danger — \"increased lens thickness → **pupil block** → elevated IOP\". `L18) Cataract II` lists the consequence among the **URGENT / MANDATORY** indications for surgery: \"**Phacomorphic glaucoma — swollen lens causing angle closure**\", and its summary row reads \"Phacomorphic Glaucoma · **Immature cataract** · Swollen lens → angle closure · Red eye + pain + HM · Raised IOP, corneal oedema · **Urgent phaco**.\" The deck names the operation in the row itself.\n\n**Why the swollen lens is a good phaco case despite being an emergency.** It is an **immature** cataract — swollen because it has imbibed fluid, not because it has hardened — so its nucleus is of ordinary or even soft consistency and emulsifies easily. Its zonules are intact. Its capsule is intact, though under tension, which is why the capsulorhexis is the difficult step (a distended bag tends to run the tear outward). Everything else about the case is standard.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong, and each fails a different requirement:**\n\n| Option | What it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **Cataract with a dislocated lens.** | Fails the **zonule** requirement. With no stable suspension and no reliable bag there is nothing to work inside; the lens is removed whole. `L18` keeps ICCE for \"**subluxated lenses**\" and \"Morgagnian cataract with **zonular weakness**\", and this bank keys exactly that at `opqb-t9-394` (box: \"Intracapsular extraction is required when lens is dislocated\") and rules phaco out at `opqb-t9-363`. |\n| **Black cataract.** | Fails the **nucleus** requirement. A black (brunescent, grade 4–5) nucleus is too hard to emulsify safely: the ultrasound time needed to break it delivers far more energy to the corneal endothelium, and endothelium does not regenerate. `L18` says ECCE is \"Still used for **very hard (grade 4–5) nuclei**\". |\n| **Dense posterior subcapsular cateract.** | A perfectly ordinary phaco case — but *preferred* implies a comparison, and nothing about a PSC makes phaco especially indicated. There is no urgency, no anatomical peculiarity and no alternative technique it is being chosen over. It is the weakest kind of wrong answer: true but not responsive. |\n\n**Note the word \"preferred\".** The question is not \"in which of these is phaco possible\" — it is possible in two of them — but in which it is the *procedure of choice*. That is the swollen lens, because it is both technically suitable and clinically urgent.\n\n*(The behaviour of a capsulorhexis in a distended bag, and the endothelial cost of long phaco times, are **not taken from the course material**; `L18` names the hardness threshold without explaining it.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'An intumescent cataract has intact zonules and a soft nucleus and threatens phacomorphic angle closure — urgent phaco; a dislocated or rock-hard lens goes another route.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.59 (Q386), key p.63' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-387', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'The **abuse of steroid eye drops** can lead to:',
    options:['Blue dot cataract.','Nuclear cataract.','Anterior polar cataract.','Posterior subcapsalar cataract.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Steroid cataract is posterior subcapsular, and both decks say so without qualification.** `L18) Cataract II`, PSC panel: \"**Causes: steroids, DM, uveitis, irradiation, myotonic dystrophy**\", with the margin warning \"**PSC + young patient + systemic steroids = Always ask about steroid use!**\". Its revision page asks it as a one-liner: \"Q: **Steroid-induced cataract pattern?** A: **PSC — Posterior SubCapsular.**\" Its summary table gives PSC the typical patient \"**Any age (steroids, DM)**\" and the special feature \"**Steroid-induced pattern**\". `L17) Cataract I` reaches the same place from the aetiological side, listing **Topical steroids** among the *local ocular* causes of **complicated cataract**, and pairing \"Thermal, **Complicated → posterior subcapsular cataract**\" in its morphological list.\n\n**Why a PSC matters more than its size suggests.** `L18`: \"Opacity at **posterior capsule (nodal point location)**\", \"**Most visually disabling per unit size** — near nodal point\", \"Worst symptoms: **near vision, bright light, miosis**\". A plaque a millimetre across sitting at the nodal point of the eye blocks more of the useful bundle than a much larger peripheral opacity would, and anything that constricts the pupil — sunlight, reading, accommodation — drives the remaining light straight through it. That is why the patient complains most in exactly the situations that ought to help: bright light and close work. `opqb-t9-397` builds the vignette.\n\n**And it is preventable.** \"Abuse\" in the stem is the word that matters: topical steroid used without supervision — for an allergic eye, a red eye, or simply repeated from an old prescription — causes both a PSC and steroid-responder glaucoma, and neither is noticed by the patient until damage is done.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What actually causes it |\n|---|---|\n| **Blue dot cataract.** | A **congenital / developmental** morphology, present from early life and usually visually insignificant — `L17` lists it among \"lamellar, **blue dot**, anterior pyramidal, posterior polar, membranous, sutural\", and `content\\ophtho\\book\\ch16-paediatric.txt` puts it in the box that \"**do not require treatment**\". Nothing to do with drugs. |\n| **Nuclear cataract.** | The **age-related** morphology — \"Exaggeration of nuclear sclerosis (yellowing → brunescence)\" — the one that gives second sight and a myopic shift. Steroids do not accelerate it. |\n| **Anterior polar cataract.** | Congenital, on the **front** surface, and usually a small localised dot (`opqb-t9-384`). Steroid opacity forms at the back of the lens, not the front. |\n\n**The pattern to remember:** *posterior* subcapsular means the opacity forms where the newest lens fibres are laid down and where the epithelium migrates — the back of the lens is where a metabolic or toxic insult shows up first.\n\n*(That PSC forms at the site of aberrant posterior migration of lens epithelium, and that steroid responders develop raised IOP as well, are **not taken from the cataract decks**; the module teaches steroid-induced glaucoma in `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry`.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Topical steroid abuse produces a posterior subcapsular cataract — the morphology that is most visually disabling per unit size because it sits at the nodal point.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.59 (Q387), key p.63' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-388', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'A **65-year-old** patient, **previously emmetropic**, now complains of defective vision **especially in the day time or when exposed to a car head light in the evening**. On examination the visual acuity was **6/18 and 6/12**, with **refraction of 4 D and 5 D** in the right and left eyes respectively. The **fundus was normal in both eyes**. This clinical picture is suggestive of:',
    options:['Mature cortical cataract.','Anterior polar cataract.','Posterior subcapsular cataract,','Nuclear cataract.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Two findings compete in this vignette, and one of them is decisive. The glare points at PSC; the refraction settles it as nuclear.**\n\n**The decisive finding is that a previously emmetropic 65-year-old now carries 4–5 D of refractive error.** `L18) Cataract II` gives that change to one morphology only: \"**Increased nuclear refractive index → acquired myopia**\", \"**:nuclear cataract → myopia, not hyperopia**\", \"Interferes predominantly with **distant** vision\", \"'**Second sight**': elderly regain near vision temporarily\". No other cataract type changes the refraction at all — a cortical spoke or a posterior plaque scatters light without altering the eye's power. So an emmetrope who has acquired several dioptres has a lens whose *index* has changed, and that is nuclear sclerosis. *(The bank prints the refractions without a sign; on this reasoning they are minus.)*\n\n**Why the glare does not overturn it.** `L18`'s comparison table grades glare across all three morphologies — Nuclear \"**Mild**\", Cortical \"Moderate\", PSC \"**SEVERE (esp. at night)**\" — so glare is present in nuclear cataract too, merely less prominent. And the deck attaches headlights to the general symptom rather than to PSC alone: \"**Glare** — Difficulty in bright light / oncoming headlights (**PSC worst**)\". *Worst* is a ranking, not an exclusive.\n\n**Why the acuities support nuclear as well.** 6/18 and 6/12 with several dioptres uncorrected is what you would expect from a distance blur; a PSC severe enough to dominate the picture would characteristically ruin **near** vision and reading (`L18`: \"Near worse (miosis aggravates)\", \"Most troubled by: **Reading & bright sunlight**\"), and the stem's complaint is about daytime and headlights, not about reading.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why the vignette excludes it |\n|---|---|\n| **Mature cortical cataract.** | A mature lens gives **HM** and an absent red reflex (`L18`: \"VA: HM only (not less!) · Red Reflex: **ABSENT**\"). This patient reads 6/12 — and the fundus was **seen** and was normal, which is impossible through a mature cataract. |\n| **Anterior polar cataract.** | A congenital, usually tiny opacity on the front of the lens (`opqb-t9-384`). It does not present for the first time at 65 and does not shift refraction by dioptres. |\n| **Posterior subcapsular cataract,** | The genuine rival on the glare symptom — but it does not change the refraction, and it would be expected to trouble near work most. It is also the morphology of **steroid, diabetic and uveitic** eyes (`L18`: \"Causes: steroids, DM, uveitis, irradiation, myotonic dystrophy\"), none of which the stem offers. |\n\n**The family of nuclear-cataract questions in this topic** — `opqb-t9-360` (mechanism), `opqb-t9-362` (the refraction arithmetic), `opqb-t9-398` and `opqb-t9-407` (second sight vignettes), `opqb-t9-402` (acquired simple myopia) — and the existing `opqb-t1-70`. This one is the version that gives you both the symptom and the number and asks you to weigh them.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A previously emmetropic patient who has acquired several dioptres of myopia has nuclear sclerosis; glare is present in every cataract and is not by itself diagnostic of PSC.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.59 (Q388), key p.63' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-389', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'**Complicated cataract** may result from the following ocular diseases **except**:',
    options:['High myopia.','Anterior uveitis.','Acute congestive glaucoma.','Paralytic squint.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**\"Complicated\" cataract means a lens that has opacified because of another disease in or of the eye, and the disease has to be one that changes the lens's environment. A paralytic squint changes nothing inside the eye.**\n\n`L17) Cataract I` prints the aetiological list:\n\n> \"**Complicated Cataract** — **Local ocular**: **High myopia** · **Anterior uveitis** · **Topical steroids**. **Systemic**: **Diabetes** · **Cushing syndrome** · **Hyperthyroidism**.\"\n\n**Two of the four options are on that list by name.** `L18) Cataract II` adds the morphology and the mechanism in its summary row: \"**Complicated** · Systemic/ocular disease · **Usually PSC** · Variable · **Posterior synechiae (uveitis)** · Treat cause first\", and lists uveitis among the causes of PSC.\n\n**Why a paralytic squint cannot do it.** A paralytic squint is a failure of an **extraocular** muscle or the nerve supplying it — third, fourth or sixth cranial nerve palsy. The globe is intact, the anterior chamber undisturbed, the aqueous normal, the lens metabolism untouched. The eye is merely pointed the wrong way. It produces diplopia and an abnormal head posture, not lens opacity. *(A childhood **sensory** squint is a different animal and is a **consequence** of an opaque lens, not a cause — `content\\ophtho\\book\\ch16-paediatric.txt`: \"**unilateral** congenital cataract can lead to **sensory squint**\", and `opqb-t9-374` is built on exactly that history.)*\n\n**Why the other three do cause it:**\n\n| Option | Route to lens opacity |\n|---|---|\n| **High myopia.** | Printed by `L17` under local ocular causes. A long, degenerate eye has abnormal vitreous and aqueous dynamics and altered lens nutrition; myopes both develop cataract earlier and characteristically develop **nuclear** and posterior subcapsular changes. `L18` also lists high myopia among the eyes where biometry needs \"modern formulae for outlier eyes\". |\n| **Anterior uveitis.** | The classic, printed in both decks. Inflammatory cells and protein in the aqueous poison lens metabolism; **posterior synechiae** bind iris to the anterior capsule; and the treatment — topical steroids — is itself a cause. Uveitic cataract is characteristically **PSC**. |\n| **Acute congestive glaucoma.** | Angle-closure with a very high pressure damages the lens epithelium and leaves **glaukomflecken** — small anterior subcapsular white flecks — which is why an acute attack is followed by lens opacity. The pressure and the ischaemia are the mechanism. *(The eponym is **not taken from the course material**; `L17` names acute congestive glaucoma nowhere in the cataract deck, but the module teaches the attack itself in `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry`.)* |\n\n**Note that the answer is the only option in the list that is not intraocular at all.** When a question offers three intraocular diseases and one extraocular one, the axis being tested is usually exactly that.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Complicated cataract follows disease that alters the lens’s environment — high myopia, uveitis, acute angle closure; a paralytic squint is extraocular and cannot.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.59 (Q389), key p.63' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-390', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'Before the decision of **cataract extraction and intraocular lens implantation**, the followings must be done **except**:',
    options:['Fundus examination','Ultrasonography.','Electrophysiological tests.','Computerized tomography.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Three of these four are on `L18) Cataract II`'s pre-operative checklist. The fourth is not, and is not an eye test at all in this context.**\n\n`L18`'s *Pre-Operative Assessment* slide, \"Posterior Segment Evaluation\": \"**B-scan ultrasound if fundus not visible** → rule out retinal detachment / tumour · **Electroretinogram (ERG) if macular disease suspected** · **OCT** when possible in hypermature cataract\". Its *Clinical Approach* slide adds the fourth block: \"**Fundal Examination** — Dilated fundus exam with +90D or indirect · Macula: AMD, diabetic maculopathy, ERM · Disc: glaucomatous cupping? · Periphery: retinal breaks, lattice degeneration · **If not visible → B-scan ultrasound mandatory.**\"\n\n**Computed tomography images bone, orbit and brain.** Its ophthalmic indications are orbital trauma and a suspected intraocular or intraorbital **foreign body**, orbital masses, and proptosis — the territory of `L10) orbit` and `L25) Trauma I` / `L26) Trauma II`. It cannot resolve the retina, cannot assess retinal function, and adds ionising radiation for no information the cataract surgeon needs. Nothing in either cataract deck mentions it.\n\n**Why the other three are required, and the logic that orders them:**\n\n| Option | What it establishes | When |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Fundus examination** | Whether the macula, disc and periphery are healthy — i.e. what the eye is capable of after surgery. | Always, and first, **if the media allow**. |\n| **Ultrasonography.** | Whether the retina is attached and the vitreous cavity is clear, when the fundus cannot be seen. | When the fundus is not visible — `opqb-t9-371`, `opqb-t9-376`. |\n| **Electrophysiological tests.** | Whether the retina **works**, when neither look nor ultrasound can say. | With very poor acuity or suspected macular disease — `opqb-t9-377`, `opqb-t9-385`. |\n\n**The sequence is the point:** look if you can; if you cannot look, listen with sound; if sound shows an attached retina but you still cannot predict the outcome, measure the electricity. CT sits outside that chain entirely.\n\n**A note on the word \"must\".** The deck qualifies two of the three — ultrasound \"**if** fundus not visible\", ERG \"**if** macular disease suspected\" — so on the strictest reading none of the three is universally mandatory. The question is asking which one is never part of the work-up at all, and only CT answers that.\n\n*(The indications for orbital CT are **not taken from the cataract decks**; the module teaches them under orbit and trauma.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The cataract work-up is fundus examination, then B-scan when the fundus cannot be seen, then electrophysiology for function; CT belongs to orbital and trauma imaging.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.59 (Q390), key p.63' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-391', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'The **worst complication** that may happen after **cataract surgery** is:',
    options:['Corneal edema.','Iris prolapse.','Endophthalmitis.','Increased ocular tension.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**`L18) Cataract II` uses the superlative itself.** Its postoperative complications panel introduces endophthalmitis as \"**Most serious vision-threatening complication**\", and its revision page asks it as a one-liner: \"Q: **Most serious post-op complication?** A: **Endophthalmitis** — intravitreal vancomycin + ceftazidime.\"\n\n**Why it outranks the other three: it is the only one that can destroy the eye rather than damage it.** Bacteria multiplying in an avascular vitreous cavity meet no immune response worth the name and produce toxins that kill photoreceptors within hours. `L18`: \"**24–72 hrs** (acute) or weeks (delayed) · Causative agents: **CoNS (Staphylococcus epidermidis most common)**, S. aureus, streptococci · Signs: **pain, redness, reduced VA, hypopyon, yellowish vitreous haze** · Tx: **urgent vitreous tap + intravitreal antibiotics (vancomycin + ceftazidime)**. Severe: **vitrectomy**.\" A delay of a day can be the difference between 6/12 and no perception of light, which is why `opqb-t9-355` — printed twice, on p.54 and again on p.60 — frames it as the *first* suspicion rather than the likeliest diagnosis.\n\n**Note the axis: \"worst\" is severity, not frequency.** The commonest complication is **PCO** — `L18`: \"Most common late complication\" — which is treated in a clinic chair with a YAG laser (`opqb-t9-349`, `opqb-t9-380`), and this bank keys that ranking separately at `opqb-t9-365`. A question that offers both rankings in one topic is testing whether the two are being confused.\n\n**Why the other three are less serious:**\n\n| Option | What it costs, and why it is recoverable |\n|---|---|\n| **Corneal edema.** | Common and usually transient in the first days. Its bad end — bullous keratopathy from permanent endothelial loss — is serious, but it is treatable: `L18` gives \"Tx: **penetrating or endothelial keratoplasty (DSAEK/DMEK)**\", so the eye is restorable. |\n| **Iris prolapse.** | A wound complication — iris tissue presenting through an unsealed incision. It requires prompt return to theatre to reposit the iris and secure the wound, and its real danger is that it is a route **for** endophthalmitis. Repaired, the eye usually does well. |\n| **Increased ocular tension.** | Common early, from retained viscoelastic or inflammatory debris (`L18` lists \"Also: **retained viscoelastic, raised IOP**\", and \"**Raised IOP (transient or glaucoma)**\" among other complications). Usually transient and medically controllable. |\n\n**The one-line hierarchy for the exam:** *most common = PCO; most serious = endophthalmitis.* Everything else on the list sits between them.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Endophthalmitis is the most serious complication of cataract surgery — the one that can destroy the eye — while PCO is the most common.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.59 (Q391), key p.63' }

,

  /* opqb-t9-392 WAS FOLDED, NOT WRITTEN -- 2026-08-24.
     A within-topic reprint of `opqb-t9-355` (book p.54). Same stem word for word
     bar "oedema"/"edema", the SAME FOUR OPTIONS REORDERED, the same answer -- so
     the printed key letter moves from C on p.54 to B on p.60.
     REORDERED IS NOT REPLACED: a reordered option list is the same question, and
     a replaced distractor would have made a new one. Held once, no `alsoIn`.
     Marker: unchanged. Unlike the topic-8 fold, the FIRST printing is the boxed
     one, so the held entry is already correctly unmarked; it was this folded
     printing that carried the marker. */
  { id:'opqb-t9-393', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'The **commonest complication of aphakia after congenital cataract** is:',
    options:['Development of nystagmus.','Retinal detachment.','Amblyopia.','Macular degeneration.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"*(Secondary chapter: `op-squint` — amblyopia and its treatment are taught with squint; the entry is filed here because the question is about the aphakic eye after cataract surgery.)*\n\n**An infant's visual pathway is still being built, and it is built by clear images. Take the lens out and leave the eye uncorrected — even for weeks — and the pathway does not develop.** `content\\ophtho\\book\\ch16-paediatric.txt`, *Paediatric cataract* tutorial box: \"**Visual rehabilitation after surgery:** Patients require treatment with eyeglasses, bifocals, or contact lenses, & eye-patching. **Often, pediatric cataracts result in some degree of amblyopia.**\" The same chapter lists among the mechanisms of amblyopia \"**Form deprivation and occlusion amblyopia** (Opacities in ocular media such as cataract…)\", and gives \"**Cataracts**\" first in its own list of causes. `L18) Cataract II` gives paediatric cataract the key VA finding \"**VA loss + amblyopia risk**\".\n\n**Why aphakia specifically.** A child left aphakic is **+10 to +12 D hypermetropic** (`L18`: \"Refractive error — +10 to +12 D hypermetropia (previously emmetropic eye)\") and has no accommodation at all, so without a contact lens or spectacles the retinal image is hopelessly out of focus. That is form deprivation just as surely as the cataract was. `L18` sets out the paediatric correction: \"**Contact lens preferred < 2 years**; secondary IOL when eye growth stabilises (~2 years)\". Removing the cataract is the easy half; keeping the eye in focus afterwards is the half that determines the result.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Where it belongs |\n|---|---|\n| **Development of nystagmus.** | A real outcome, but of **bilateral** untreated congenital cataract and of deprivation in the first weeks of life — the book: \"**bilateral** congenital cataract can lead to **nystagmus** with poor visual prognosis\". It is a consequence of bilateral deprivation before fixation is established, not the commonest complication of aphakia generally. |\n| **Retinal detachment.** | A recognised **late** risk in eyes operated in childhood, especially highly myopic ones and those with capsular rupture (`L18`: \"Posterior capsule rupture + vitreous loss (intraop) → higher risk of retinal detachment\"). Serious, but uncommon compared with amblyopia, which is close to universal without diligent rehabilitation. |\n| **Macular degeneration.** | An age-related disease of the elderly retina. It has no connection with paediatric aphakia; `L18` lists **AMD** only in the pre-operative fundus checklist for adults. |\n\n**The clinical rule this question encodes:** in a child, the operation is a small part of the treatment. The corrections, the patching regimen and the follow-up are what save the vision — which is why `opqb-t9-364` puts the paediatrician and `opqb-t9-384` puts serial acuity testing at the centre of paediatric cataract management.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'An uncorrected aphakic child is +10 to +12 D out of focus with no accommodation, so amblyopia is the commonest complication after congenital cataract surgery.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.60 (Q393), key p.63' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-394', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'**Intracapsular cataract extraction** is a **must** only in:',
    options:['Mature senile cortical cataract.','Immature senile cortical cataract.','Anteriorly dislocated lens.','Nuclear cataract.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"Intracapsular extraction **is required when lens is dislocated.**\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**Intracapsular means the lens comes out inside its own capsule, in one piece.** `L18) Cataract II`: \"**ICCE — Intracapsular Extraction** · Incision: **Large incision** — entire lens + capsule removed · **ENTIRE lens including capsule removed (cryo or forceps)** · **Historical procedure — rarely performed today** · No capsular bag → IOL placed in **anterior chamber (ACIOL)** · High rate of complications: **bullous keratopathy, glaucoma, vitreous prolapse** · **Still performed in Morgagnian cataract with zonular weakness** (careful selection).\"\n\n**The word \"must\" is the whole question.** ICCE is not the preferred operation for anything — the deck calls it historical and lists a formidable complication rate. It survives for one situation: **when the zonules will not hold the capsule**. Every other technique depends on the capsular bag staying suspended while the surgeon works inside it. A lens that has already come loose and fallen forward into the anterior chamber has no such suspension; the only safe manoeuvre is to lift the whole thing out. That is the *must*.\n\n**Why an anteriorly dislocated lens is also urgent as well as technically special:** in the anterior chamber it blocks the pupil directly, closes the angle, and drives the pressure up — `L18` lists \"**Subluxated lens with acute pupillary block**\" among the **urgent/mandatory** indications for surgery, and it lies against the corneal endothelium, which does not regenerate. `opqb-t9-352` asks the pressure consequence and `opqb-t9-363` rules phacoemulsification out for the same anatomy.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — and note that all three are ordinary lenses with intact zonules:**\n\n| Option | The correct operation |\n|---|---|\n| **Mature senile cortical cataract.** | **Phaco** in modern practice — a white lens is technically harder (no red reflex to stain the rhexis against) but the bag and zonules are intact. |\n| **Immature senile cortical cataract.** | The easiest phaco case there is. |\n| **Nuclear cataract.** | Phaco, unless the nucleus is grade 4–5 black, in which case **ECCE** — `L18`: \"Still used for **very hard (grade 4–5) nuclei**\" — and this bank keys ECCE + IOL for a hard nuclear cataract at `opqb-t9-400`. Not ICCE either way, because the capsule can still be preserved. |\n\n**The decision tree the topic keeps testing:** capsule and zonules intact + nucleus emulsifiable → **phaco**; capsule intact but nucleus rock-hard → **ECCE**; capsule unsupported or lens dislocated → **ICCE** or lensectomy.",
    objective:'ICCE is obsolete except where the zonules cannot hold the capsule — a dislocated or subluxated lens must come out whole, inside its capsule.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.60 (Q394), key p.63 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-395', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'**Phacoemulsification** of the cataractous lens is indicated in the following conditions, **except**:',
    options:['Mature senile cortical cataract.','Immature senile cortical cataract.','Early nuclear cataract.','Congenital cataract.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Phacoemulsification exists to break up a hard nucleus. A child's lens has no hard nucleus, so there is nothing to emulsify.**\n\n`L17) Cataract I` divides the operation by age in one line: \"Surgery (Lens extraction) — **Children → irrigation / aspiration (soft lens)** · **Adults → conventional ECCE, small incision cataract extraction (phacoemulsification)**.\" The parenthesis *(soft lens)* is the reason. `L17` explains the consistency earlier: \"**Young age → fluid** · Older age → center solid ('**nucleus**'), surrounding periphery fluid ('cortex')\". A nucleus is something that forms with age; an infant's lens is uniformly soft and can simply be irrigated and aspirated out through a small port, with no ultrasound energy at all. `L18) Cataract II` gives paediatric cataract the surgery column \"**Aspiration ± IOL**\".\n\n**Why using ultrasound on a soft lens would be actively wrong**, not merely unnecessary: phaco power delivered into a lens that offers no resistance releases energy into the anterior chamber and against the corneal endothelium, and the paediatric capsule is elastic and tears unpredictably. The paediatric operation is also different at the back — the posterior capsule is usually opened and an anterior vitrectomy performed at the same sitting, because a child's capsule opacifies within months and a small child cannot cooperate with a YAG laser.\n\n**Why the other three are phaco cases:**\n\n| Option | Why phaco suits it |\n|---|---|\n| **Mature senile cortical cataract.** | Harder than routine — dense, no red reflex — but zonules and bag are intact, and this is standard modern practice. Only a black grade 4–5 nucleus sends the surgeon to ECCE. |\n| **Immature senile cortical cataract.** | The routine case. `L18` calls phaco the \"**Gold standard worldwide** for routine cataract surgery\". |\n| **Early nuclear cataract.** | Soft nucleus, minimal ultrasound energy, quick case. The only question in an early cataract is whether to operate at all, and `L18` answers it functionally: \"Visual impairment affecting **quality of life** (driving, reading, ADL)\", \"VA below driving standard (typically **<6/12** in driving eye)\". |\n\n**Compare `opqb-t9-386`**, which asks the same axis from the positive side and keys the intumescent cataract, and `opqb-t9-363`, which rules phaco out for a dislocated lens. Three questions, one rule: **phaco needs intact zonules, an intact bag, and a nucleus worth emulsifying.**\n\n*(That the paediatric operation includes a primary posterior capsulotomy and anterior vitrectomy is **not taken from the course material**; the decks give only \"irrigation / aspiration\" and \"Aspiration ± IOL\".)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A child’s lens is soft and is removed by irrigation/aspiration; phacoemulsification is for the adult lens with a nucleus to emulsify.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.60 (Q395), key p.63' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-396', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'A **78-year-old** patient complained of **mild difficulty in reading and seeing street signs**. Her vision in the right eye is **6/24** and the left eye **6/18**, with **no improvement on pin hole** in both eyes. The **cornea was bilaterally clear**. On **direct ophthalmoscopy there was difficulty in seeing fundus details** in both eyes. What is your diagnosis?',
    options:['Myopia and Presbyopia','Hyperopia and Presbyopia.','Astigmatism and Presbyopia.','Cataractors changes in the lens'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Three findings, and each of them eliminates the refractive options one after another.**\n\n**1. No improvement with the pinhole.** `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination`, slide 119: \"**If VA improves, then the visual impairment is due to refractive error** (should be correctable with spectacles or a new prescription). **If VA worsens, another ocular pathology is causing the visual impairment.**\" The book puts it the same way (`content\\ophtho\\book\\ch01-examination.txt`, *Pinhole test*): \"If VA **improves** the pinhole than that recorded before then the patient has a **refractive error** that requires correction with glasses.\" A pinhole works by cutting out the peripheral rays that a defocused eye cannot converge; it cannot help an eye whose problem is that the light is being scattered rather than misdirected. **No pinhole improvement therefore excludes options A, B and C in one stroke**, because all three are refractive errors.\n\n**2. Difficulty seeing fundus details on direct ophthalmoscopy, with a clear cornea.** The examiner's own view is obstructed, and the obstruction is not in the cornea because the stem says so. Between cornea and retina there is one structure left that can go cloudy: the lens. `L18) Cataract II` uses the same reasoning in reverse for its pre-operative rule — \"If not visible → B-scan ultrasound mandatory\".\n\n**3. The symptoms fit.** Both distance (street signs) and near (reading) are affected, mildly, in a 78-year-old — `L17) Cataract I`'s \"**Gradual progressive painless diminution of vision**\", and `L18`'s \"Senile cataract is the most common cause of **reversible blindness** in the world\", starting \"from the 5th decade onwards\".\n\n**Why the reading difficulty is not the answer.** Presbyopia is universal at 78 and the patient certainly has it, but presbyopia does not reduce **distance** acuity to 6/24 and does not obstruct the examiner's view of the fundus. Options A, B and C each pair presbyopia with a distance error; the pinhole result says the distance loss is not refractive.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong in its own right:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Myopia and Presbyopia** | A myope's distance blur improves markedly with a pinhole. It also does not obscure the examiner's fundus view — a highly myopic fundus is *easier* to see with a direct ophthalmoscope, merely magnified differently. |\n| **Hyperopia and Presbyopia.** | Same pinhole objection. And an uncorrected hyperope of 78 has been struggling for decades; the stem describes a change. |\n| **Astigmatism and Presbyopia.** | The pinhole is at its most dramatic in astigmatism, because it removes the meridional blur almost entirely. \"No improvement\" excludes it most firmly of the three. |\n\n**The printed answer, \"Cataractors changes in the lens\", is a mis-set \"cataractous changes\"** — the option is kept as printed.\n\n*(Why a pinhole cannot help a scattering media, as distinct from a defocused one, is **not taken from the course material**; the decks state the interpretation without the optics.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Vision that does not improve with a pinhole is not refractive; a clear cornea with a poor fundus view localises the opacity to the lens.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.60 (Q396), key p.63' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-397', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'A patient aged **55 years** suffers from **defective vision on working with a computer**. His **far vision is 6/12**. Which type of cataract do you expect to find on **slit lamp examination**?',
    options:['Nuclear cataract.','Mature cortical cataract.','Posterior sub capsular.','Anterior polar cataract.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Near work worse than distance is the signature of a posterior subcapsular cataract, and the reason is the pupil.**\n\n`L18) Cataract II`, PSC panel: \"Opacity at **posterior capsule (nodal point location)** · **Most visually disabling per unit size — near nodal point** · **Worst symptoms: near vision, bright light, miosis** · Appears dark against red reflex (retroillumination) · Causes: **steroids, DM, uveitis, irradiation, myotonic dystrophy**.\" Its comparison table: \"Near/Dist vision — **Near worse (miosis aggravates)**\"; \"Most troubled by — **Reading & bright sunlight**\"; and its revision page, \"Q: **PSC → which condition worst?** A: **Near vision in BRIGHT LIGHT** (miosis brings opaque area into visual axis).\"\n\n**The mechanism in one sentence:** looking at something close triggers the near reflex, and the near reflex constricts the pupil; a constricted pupil forces every ray through the centre of the lens, which is exactly where the posterior plaque sits. So the more the patient tries to see near, the more completely the opacity is in the way.\n\n**The two numbers in the stem confirm it.** The **distance** acuity is 6/12 — only mildly reduced — while the *complaint* is entirely about close work at a screen. A lesion that impairs near far out of proportion to distance is a nodal-point lesion. And the age, **55**, is younger than typical senile cataract, which fits the deck's PSC column \"Typical Patient: **Any age (steroids, DM)**\"; a PSC in a patient of 55 is a reason to ask about steroid use and to check a blood sugar (`opqb-t9-387`, `opqb-t9-403`).\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why the pattern does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| **Nuclear cataract.** | The opposite pattern. `L18`: \"Interferes predominantly with **distant** vision\", \"'**Second sight**': elderly regain near vision temporarily\", table row \"Distant worse (myopic shift)\", \"Most troubled by: **Driving / TV (distance)**\". A nuclear cataract makes near work *easier* for a while. |\n| **Mature cortical cataract.** | A mature lens gives **HM** and an absent red reflex. This patient reads 6/12 at distance and is complaining about a computer screen — nowhere near mature. |\n| **Anterior polar cataract.** | A congenital dot on the **front** of the lens, usually visually insignificant (`opqb-t9-384`). It does not present as a new near-vision complaint in a 55-year-old. |\n\n**A caution worth holding:** at 55 this man is also presbyopic, and a presbyope at a computer screen — an intermediate working distance — is a very common non-cataract complaint. What makes the cataract answer right here is that the question explicitly asks what will be found **on slit lamp examination**, i.e. it stipulates that there is a lens opacity and asks which.\n\n*(The near-reflex account of why miosis worsens a PSC is a reading of `L18`'s own \"miosis brings opaque area into visual axis\"; the physiological triad of the near reflex is taught in `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway`.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A posterior subcapsular cataract sits at the nodal point, so miosis during near work and bright light drives the symptoms while distance acuity stays relatively good.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.60 (Q397), key p.63' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-398', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'A **45-year-old emmetropic** driver **using reading glasses** noticed recently that he **can read without glasses** while he **can’t see far street signs**. What is the type of cataract you expect to see on **slit lamp examination**?',
    options:['Anterior subcapsular cataract','Cortical cataract.','Nuclear cataract.','Posterior subcapsular cataract.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"Myopic shift in emmetropes is caused by **nuclear sclerosis**\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**This is \"second sight\", and it is the one symptom in ophthalmology where a patient gets *better* at something while going blind.** `L18) Cataract II` prints it twice: in the symptom list, \"'**Second sight**' — **Nuclear: myopic shift allows reading without glasses (temporary)**\", and in the nuclear panel, \"**Increased nuclear refractive index → acquired myopia**\", \"**Interferes predominantly with distant vision**\", \"'Second sight': **elderly regain near vision temporarily**\". Its revision page: \"Q: Nuclear cataract → which vision affected most? A: **DISTANT vision (myopic shift). Patient may read better ('second sight')**.\"\n\n**Why gaining myopia restores reading.** A presbyope has lost the ability to add plus power for near, which is why he needs reading glasses. A nuclear cataract *adds the plus power for him*, by raising the refractive index of the lens nucleus — so the eye is now permanently focused a little closer than infinity. Near objects come into focus without glasses; distant ones no longer do. The patient throws away his reading glasses and finds he cannot read the road signs. The gain is temporary, because the lens keeps opacifying.\n\n**The trap the stem lays.** Reading better sounds like an improvement, and the word *emmetropic* invites you to think nothing has changed refractively. Both point at the answer: the change **is** refractive, and it is in the myopic direction, which only nuclear sclerosis produces.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it cannot produce this history |\n|---|---|\n| **Anterior subcapsular cataract** | An opacity on the **front** of the lens. It scatters light and, like any axial opacity, would make things worse in both directions; it does not shift the refraction. |\n| **Cortical cataract.** | `L18`: \"Begins as **wedge-shaped / spoke-like peripheral opacities**\", \"Impact on VA depends on **proximity to visual axis**\", \"Glare prominent when opacities are equatorial\". Cortical spokes cause glare and variable blur — never a myopic shift, because they alter no index on the axis. |\n| **Posterior subcapsular cataract.** | The exact opposite complaint. PSC is \"**Near worse (miosis aggravates)**\", \"Most troubled by **Reading & bright sunlight**\". A PSC patient loses his reading first; this one has just regained it. `opqb-t9-397` is the PSC version of the same question. |\n\n**⚠️ The same idea appears five times in this topic**, and it is worth reading them together: mechanism at `opqb-t9-360`, the refraction arithmetic at `opqb-t9-362`, an emmetrope acquiring simple myopia at `opqb-t9-402`, a second reading-without-glasses vignette at `opqb-t9-407`, and a glare-plus-refraction case at `opqb-t9-388`. The existing `opqb-t1-70` asks it as \"second sight\" in a myope.\n\n*(The account of why added plus power substitutes for accommodation is **not taken from the course material**; the deck states the phenomenon and its cause without the optics.)*",
    objective:'A presbyope who suddenly reads without glasses but loses distance vision has second sight from nuclear sclerosis — the myopic shift of a rising nuclear refractive index.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.60 (Q398), key p.63 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-399', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'An **emmetropic diabetic** patient who is on **medical control**. He suffered lately from **defective vision that did not improve using the pin hole test**. What would you like to do?',
    options:['Fit him with far glasses.','Fit him with near glasses','Fit him with contact lens.','Detailed fundus examination.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"Pin-hole test failed; suspect retinal pathology rather than refractive error.\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-ret-dr` — what the fundus examination is looking for is diabetic retinopathy; the entry is filed in the lens topic because that is where the bank prints it and because the pinhole is the test that clears the media and the refraction out of the way.)*\n\n**Three of the four options are ways of putting a lens in front of the eye, and the stem has already told you that lenses will not help.** That is the whole question.\n\n**What the pinhole does.** `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination`, slide 118: \"If 6/6 is not achieved, repeat VA testing using a **pinhole occluder** (with or without spectacles). The use of the pinhole **reduces the need to focus light entering the eye**.\" A narrow aperture admits only the central pencil of rays, which passes through the nodal region undeviated, so it reaches the retina in focus whatever the eye's refractive error. The pinhole is, in effect, a spectacle lens of every power at once.\n\n**How its result is read — and note that three sources set the ceiling on the answer at three different widths.** Slide 119: \"If VA **improves**, then the visual impairment is due to **refractive error**… If VA **worsens**, **another ocular pathology** is causing the visual impairment.\" `content\\ophtho\\book\\ch01-examination.txt` sets a threshold and then narrows the conclusion: \"If the recorded visual acuity is **6/12 or less** in one or both eyes, repeat the test with the patient viewing the test chart through a pinhole occluder… If VA **improves**… the patient has a **refractive error**… ⚠️ If vision get worse, it indicates **macular lesion**\". The bank's own box narrows it a third way, to \"**retinal pathology**\". All three agree on the direction of the inference, and the key does not depend on which width you take: a pinhole that does not improve vision has excluded refraction as the explanation.\n\n**Why the stem says \"emmetropic\" and \"on medical control\".** Both words exist to make the refractive options tempting. An emmetrope of any age eventually needs a reading correction, and a diabetic's refraction genuinely does move — `L23) Ocular manifestations of systemic diseases`: \"Diabetes mellitus may have various ocular complications including **refractive changes** and **cataract formation**, but **the most important is retinopathy**.\" A blood-sugar swing shifts the lens's hydration and hence its power, and that shift *is* correctable with glasses. The pinhole result is what tells you this is not that.\n\n**What \"detailed fundus examination\" means.** `L18) Cataract II`, clinical approach: \"**Dilated fundus exam with +90D or indirect** · Macula: **AMD, diabetic maculopathy, ERM** · Disc: glaucomatous cupping? · Periphery: retinal breaks, lattice degeneration · **If not visible → B-scan ultrasound mandatory**.\" In a diabetic with unexplained visual loss the target is maculopathy and proliferative disease, and `L18` lists among the elective indications for cataract surgery \"To facilitate management of posterior segment disease (e.g., **diabetic retinopathy requiring laser/OCT monitoring**)\" — the retina outranks the lens in this patient in both directions.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Fit him with far glasses.** | A distance correction treats a distance refractive error. The pinhole is a universal refractive correction and it produced no improvement, so no spectacle lens will. |\n| **Fit him with near glasses** | Same objection, and the stem does not describe a near complaint at all. |\n| **Fit him with contact lens.** | A contact lens is the same optical correction moved to the corneal plane. It changes magnification and vertex distance, not the fact that refraction is not the problem. |\n\n**The rule to carry:** *pinhole improves → refract; pinhole does not improve → look inside.* `opqb-t9-396` runs the identical logic in an elderly patient and lands on the lens; here the media are clear and the same logic lands on the retina.",
    objective:'A pinhole is a universal refractive correction, so vision that does not improve through it is not refractive — in a diabetic that mandates a dilated fundus examination, not a prescription.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.61 (Q399), key p.63 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-400', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'**Extracapsular cataract extraction + IOL** is the **operation of choice** in:',
    options:['Anteriorly dislocated lens.','Hard nuclear cataract','Posterior dislocated lens.','Anterior polar cataract.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Extracapsular means the nucleus comes out and the posterior capsule stays behind**, and that surviving capsule is what makes the \"+ IOL\" half of the stem possible. `L18) Cataract II`: \"**ECCE — Extracapsular Extraction** · Incision: **8–10 mm limbal incision** · Manual **expression of nucleus** through anterior capsulotomy · **Posterior capsule LEFT IN PLACE (supports IOL)** · **RIGID PMMA IOL implanted** (doesn't fold) · Requires 10/0 nylon wound sutures → **significant astigmatism** · **Still used for very hard (grade 4–5) nuclei**, subluxated lenses, resource-limited settings.\"\n\n**Why hardness is the deciding variable.** Phacoemulsification is the standard operation — `L18` marks it STANDARD — and it works by ultrasonically emulsifying the nucleus so it can be aspirated through a 2.2–3.0 mm incision. A grade 4–5 brunescent nucleus resists emulsification: the energy and time needed to break it up rise steeply, and that energy is spent inside a 3 mm anterior chamber against a corneal endothelium that does not regenerate (`L18` lists \"Bullous keratopathy from **endothelial cell loss**\" among the post-operative complications, with \"Prevention: **low-energy phaco technique**\"). ECCE trades a large sutured wound and its astigmatism for delivering that nucleus in one piece with no ultrasound at all. `opqb-t9-394` is the mirror image of this question — it asks when ECCE is *not* enough.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | The right operation, and why |\n|---|---|\n| **Anteriorly dislocated lens.** | **ICCE.** A lens that has come loose has no zonular suspension, so there is no stable capsular bag to work inside and nothing to support an IOL in the bag. `L18` keeps ICCE alive for exactly this: \"Still performed in **Morgagnian cataract with zonular weakness**\", with the IOL then placed in the **anterior chamber**. This bank keys it that way at `opqb-t9-394`. |\n| **Posterior dislocated lens.** | A lens that has fallen back into the vitreous cavity cannot be reached through a limbal incision at all; it is retrieved from behind, through the pars plana, with vitrectomy instruments. `L18` names this state only as a **cause of aphakia** — \"posterior IOL dislocation into vitreous\" — and never prints the operation, so the approach here is *not taken from the course material*. What the decks do give you is enough to exclude ECCE: an extracapsular extraction depends on the lens still being in its capsule in front of the vitreous face. |\n| **Anterior polar cataract.** | A small opacity sitting on the anterior pole, on the visual axis but tiny. `L17) Cataract I` names its conical variant among the congenital/developmental morphologies — \"lamellar, blue dot, **anterior pyramidal**, posterior polar, membranous, sutural\" — and the exact phrase *anterior polar* appears in neither cataract deck. Most such opacities are visually insignificant (*not taken from the course material*), and `L17`'s own treatment tree then answers the question: \"**Observation** (functionally insignificant + no complications)\". No extraction of any kind. `opqb-t9-387` prints the same option as a distractor. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'ECCE with a posterior-chamber IOL is the operation of choice for a very hard (grade 4-5) nucleus, because the capsule survives to support the lens while the nucleus is delivered whole rather than emulsified.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.61 (Q400), key p.63' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-401', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'A patient with **unilateral cataract** had **extracapsular cataract extraction without IOL implantation**. He used **contact lenses** for correction; he is now **intolerant to contact lens**. What is the best for him?',
    options:['Secondary implantation of IOL','Use of ordinary glasses.','Change type of contact lens.','4 LASIK'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**⚠️ Option D is printed \"4 LASIK\" in the bank — a stray numeral in front of the word.** It is kept exactly as printed, in line with the rest of this topic's options; nothing in the source indicates what the 4 was meant to be.\n\n**This is unilateral aphakia, and the question is what is left after the one good non-surgical answer has failed.** `L17) Cataract I` sets out the whole hierarchy: \"**Glasses** → Binocular aphakia → suitable · **Monocular aphakia → least suitable** (image magnification ~ **30 %** → anisokonia → **binocular diplopia**). **Contact lenses** → Binocular aphakia → suitable · **Monocular aphakia → suitable** (image magnification ~ **7–10 %** → minimal anisokonia → no binocular diplopia). **IOL** → **Most physiological** (image magnification ~ **3 %** → no anisokonia).\"\n\n**Why the answer is surgical.** The obstacle in unilateral aphakia is aniseikonia — the two eyes forming images of different sizes, which the brain cannot fuse — and the only variable that changes it is **how far in front of the eye the correcting lens sits**. A spectacle at 12 mm magnifies by ~30 %; a contact lens on the cornea by 7–10 %; an IOL, sitting in the plane of the lens it replaces, by ~3 %. Once contact lenses are ruled out by intolerance, the only remaining way to shrink that number is to move the correction inside the eye. `L18) Cataract II` lists the three corrections for aphakia in the same order and names the last one explicitly: \"Correction — **Aphakic spectacles** (+10–12 D: thick, minifying, ring scotoma); **contact lens**; **secondary IOL**.\"\n\n**Why \"secondary\" is achievable here, and this is the detail the stem plants.** The original operation was **extracapsular** — `L18`: \"**Posterior capsule LEFT IN PLACE (supports IOL)**.\" The capsular bag or, failing that, the ciliary sulcus is still there to receive a lens years later. Had the eye been left aphakic by an intracapsular extraction or by trauma, the implant would have had to be angle-supported or sutured. `opqb-t9-350` asks the same hierarchy from the other end and `opqb-t9-367` works through the spectacle's defects in full; neither is repeated here.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Use of ordinary glasses.** | \"Ordinary\" glasses cannot correct aphakia at all — the eye needs **+10 to +12 D** (`L18`), which is not an ordinary prescription. Even the proper aphakic spectacle is what `L17` calls **least suitable** in a monocular case, for the 30 % magnification and the resulting binocular diplopia, and `L18` adds the **ring scotoma**. Moving from a correction that magnified 7–10 % to one that magnifies 30 % makes the fusion problem worse, not better. |\n| **Change type of contact lens.** | Worth one attempt in practice, but the stem states the patient is intolerant *to contact lens*, not to one particular lens, and the option offers no mechanism — the magnification, the handling and the corneal load are properties of wearing a contact lens on an aphakic eye, not of its material. |\n| **4 LASIK** | LASIK reshapes the **cornea** by a few dioptres. It cannot deliver +10 to +12 D, there is not enough corneal tissue to remove or add for it, and it would leave the aniseikonia untouched even if it could — a corneal correction sits at the same plane as a contact lens. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A contact-lens-intolerant unilateral aphake needs the correction moved inside the eye: secondary IOL implantation, which the retained posterior capsule of the earlier ECCE makes possible.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.61 (Q401), key p.63' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-402', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'A **60-year-old patient** who was **previously emmetropic** with a **healthy fundus** developed **gradual progressive simple myopia**. The expected cause is:',
    options:['Cortical cataract.','Nuclear cataracts.','Chronic simple glaucoma.','Keratoconus'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Acquired myopia in a previously emmetropic sixty-year-old with a normal fundus is nuclear sclerosis until proved otherwise.** `L18) Cataract II`, nuclear cataract panel: \"Exaggeration of nuclear sclerosis (yellowing → brunescence) · **Increased nuclear refractive index → acquired myopia** · Interferes predominantly with **distant** vision · '**Second sight**': elderly regain near vision temporarily\", with the margin note \"**:nuclear cataract → myopia, not hyperopia**\".\n\n`opqb-t9-360` asks the mechanism directly and `opqb-t9-398` carries this topic's full account of the myopic shift and of second sight; neither is repeated here. In one line: a denser nucleus is a stronger plus lens at unchanged shape and unchanged axial length, so the focus moves in front of the retina.\n\n**The three words the stem uses to close every other door.** *Previously emmetropic* rules out the myopia of growth, which is established by early adult life. *Gradual progressive* fits an opacity that is deepening, and rules out the abrupt refractive swings of hyperglycaemia. *Healthy fundus* rules out the retinal causes of visual loss and, with them, the temptation to look past the lens.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it does not produce this refraction |\n|---|---|\n| **Cortical cataract.** | Cortical opacity is **peripheral and spoke-shaped** — `L18`: \"Begins as **wedge-shaped / spoke-like peripheral opacities** · Appear **dark against the red reflex** · Impact on VA depends on **proximity to the visual axis** · **Glare prominent** when opacities are equatorial.\" It scatters light and causes glare; it does not raise the refractive index of the axial lens, so it produces no systematic myopic shift. |\n| **Chronic simple glaucoma.** | A disease of the optic nerve and the field, not of refraction. It would also fail the stem twice over: `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` builds the diagnosis on **disc cupping**, which a *healthy fundus* excludes, and its visual loss is peripheral and unnoticed rather than a change of spectacle power. |\n| **Keratoconus** | Does cause acquired myopia, and is a genuine differential — but it is a **corneal ectasia of adolescence and early adult life** that produces **irregular astigmatism**, not the *simple* myopia the stem specifies, and it is not a disease that begins at sixty. `L15,16) Cornea` teaches it as a progressive corneal thinning with a conical apex; the stem's *simple myopia* is the word that excludes it. The age of onset is *not taken from the course material*. |\n\n**The family this question belongs to:** `opqb-t9-360` (mechanism), `opqb-t9-398` and `opqb-t9-407` (second sight in a presbyope). The bank prints the same idea four times in one topic, which is a fair statement of how often it is examined.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A rising nuclear refractive index makes the lens a stronger plus lens, so nuclear cataract is the cause of acquired simple myopia in a previously emmetropic elderly patient with a normal fundus.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.61 (Q402), key p.63' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-403', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'A patient **35years old** suffered lately from **gradual painless defective vision**, **recurrent styes** and **peripheral neuropathy**. On slit lamp examination there was **very faint cataract**. Intraocular pressure was **15mmHg**. What would you like to examine most?',
    options:['Extra ocular muscle movement.','Angle of the anterior chamber.','Fundus examination.','Field of vision.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"*(Secondary chapter: `op-ret-dr` — the diagnosis the stem is steering towards is diabetes, and what the fundus examination is looking for is retinopathy; filed in the lens topic because the bank prints it there and because the *very faint* cataract is the finding the student is meant to look past.)*\n\n**Every clue in this stem points at one systemic disease, and the lens finding is the decoy.** A cataract at 35 is early. Recurrent styes and a peripheral neuropathy have nothing to do with the lens — but they have everything to do with why the lens is cloudy at 35.\n\n**Diabetes, assembled from the stem:**\n\n| Clue | Where it fits |\n|---|---|\n| **Cataract at 35** | `L17) Cataract I` classifies cataract aetiologically and puts **Diabetes** first among the **systemic** causes of **complicated cataract** (with Cushing syndrome and hyperthyroidism). A cataract in the fourth decade is not age-related — `L17`: \"Senile cataract starts from the **5th decade** onwards\" — so it is asking to be explained. |\n| **Peripheral neuropathy** | `L23) Ocular manifestations of systemic diseases` names it as a diabetic complication in the same breath as the eye: \"**Microvascular complications as neuropathy and nephropathy** can be lowered by tight glycemic control.\" |\n| **Recurrent styes** | The module gives recurrent styes to **staphylococcal blepharitis** — `content\\ophtho\\book\\ch05-eyelids.txt` lists among its complications \"Madarosis, trichiasis and poliosis · **Recurrent styes** · Chronic conjunctivitis, and phlyctens\" — and does not print the diabetic association. That recurrent lid-margin staphylococcal infection should prompt a blood sugar is *not taken from the course material*; it is standard practice, and it is plainly why the bank put the word in the stem. |\n| **Gradual painless defective vision** | The presentation of both cataract and maculopathy. The *very faint* cataract is too slight to account for it, which is the sentence's real function. |\n\n**Why the fundus is what you examine.** `L23`: \"Diabetes mellitus may have various ocular complications including refractive changes and cataract formation, **but the most important is retinopathy**.\" A faint lens opacity that does not explain the vision, in a patient whose systemic clues say diabetes, sends you through the pupil. `L18) Cataract II` gives the examination itself: \"**Dilated fundus exam with +90D or indirect** · **Macula: AMD, diabetic maculopathy, ERM** · Periphery: retinal breaks, lattice degeneration.\"\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Extra ocular muscle movement.** | Diabetes does cause cranial nerve palsies, but their complaint is **diplopia of sudden onset**, not gradual painless dimming. Nothing in the stem suggests a motility problem. |\n| **Angle of the anterior chamber.** | Gonioscopy answers a pressure question, and the stem has pre-empted it: **15 mmHg is normal**. The one diabetic disease that closes an angle is **neovascular glaucoma**, and `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` describes it as a **secondary narrow angle glaucoma** in which \"Neovascular membrane grows over the trabecular meshwork and contracts pulling the peripheral iris over the trabeculum\" — a late complication of proliferative retinopathy, with a high pressure and a red painful eye, none of which is here. Even then the finding that matters would be found in the fundus first. |\n| **Field of vision.** | A field test is the investigation for glaucoma and for lesions of the visual pathway. With a **normal pressure** and no disc information, there is nothing to test a field *for*; and diabetic maculopathy affects the **central** vision that acuity already measures. |\n\n**The habit this question is teaching:** when the ocular finding is too small for the symptom, stop looking at the finding and read the rest of the patient.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A faint cataract at 35 with recurrent styes and a peripheral neuropathy is diabetes until proved otherwise, and the most important diabetic eye complication is retinopathy — examine the fundus.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.61 (Q403), key p.63' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-404', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'A patient suffered a few months ago from **mild gradually increasing defective vision**. He was **followed up without any treatment**. **Yesterday** he suffered from **pain, red eye and marked defective vision**. His eye was **stony hard** on examination. What would you expect to find as the cause on slit lamp examination?',
    options:['Corneal ulcer.','Mutton fat Kps.','Deep anterior chamber.','Swollen intumescent lens.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"*(Secondary chapter: `op-glauc` — the acute event is an angle-closure attack; filed in the lens topic because the cause is the lens and the treatment is to remove it.)*\n\n**This is phacomorphic glaucoma, and the stem is written as a timeline: months of untreated cataract, then one night of angle closure.**\n\n**The mechanism, in the deck's own arrows.** `L17) Cataract I`, complications of cataract: \"**Intumescence** (lens fibres liquefaction and fluid imbibition → **swelling of lens**) · **Lens induced glaucoma** · **Phacomorphic** → Intumescence (lens fibres fluid imbibition) → **increased lens thickness → pupil block → elevated IOP**.\" `L18) Cataract II` prints the same entity as a row: \"**Phacomorphic Glaucoma** | Immature cataract | **Swollen lens → angle closure** | **Red eye + pain + HM** | **Raised IOP, corneal oedema** | **Urgent phaco**\", and lists it first among the **urgent/mandatory** indications for surgery: \"**Phacomorphic glaucoma — swollen lens causing angle closure**.\"\n\n**Why \"followed up without any treatment\" is in the stem.** It is not padding — it is the correct earlier decision, and it is what makes the complication possible. `L17`'s treatment tree turns on two determinants, **functional significance of the opacity** and **complications**: a *mild* opacity with no complications belongs in the **Observation** arm. The moment a complication appears, the same tree moves the patient to **Surgery**. This question is that transition, and the answer to it is not a pressure-lowering drop but taking the lens out.\n\n**Why the eye is stony hard.** `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` gives the acute angle-closure signs: \"Diminution of vision · **IOP → ++++ (stony hard)** · **Corneal oedema** · Ciliary injection · Mid-dilated vertically oval non-reactive pupil · **Shallow anterior chamber / Iris bombe** · Eyelid oedema\", with symptoms \"**Pain → ++++** · Coloured haloes · Diminution of vision → rapid (over several hours) · Redness · Nausea/Vomiting\". Every element of the stem's yesterday is on that list. A phacomorphic attack reproduces it because the swollen lens does mechanically what a narrow angle does anatomically.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Corneal ulcer.** | Painful and red, but the wrong story and the wrong pressure. An ulcer follows trauma, contact-lens wear or an epithelial defect — not months of gradually dimming vision — and it does not make an eye stony hard. |\n| **Mutton fat Kps.** | Keratic precipitates mean **anterior uveitis** — `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera`, slide 15: \"**Keratic precipitates (KPs)** on the back surface of cornea\", which is all the deck prints; the phrase *mutton fat KPs* occurs **exactly once in the whole module**, under **sympathetic ophthalmia** in `content\\ophtho\\book\\ch10-uveal-tract.txt` (printed p.140), and its own chapter records that there is \"No mutton-fat KP teaching in the anterior uveitis section\". Two problems here: uveitis is not the timeline in the stem, and it classically **lowers** the pressure by suppressing aqueous production before any secondary rise. There is also a genuine lens-induced uveitis — `L18` lists \"**Lens-induced uveitis unresponsive to medical therapy**\" among the urgent indications — but the stem's *stony hard* eye is the pressure sign, and the question asks for the **cause**, which is the lens either way. |\n| **Deep anterior chamber.** | The **opposite** of what a swollen lens produces. `L12` gives angle closure a \"**Shallow anterior chamber / Iris bombe (forward bowing)**\", and a deep chamber is a sign of **aphakia** — `L18`: \"Classic signs — Iridodonesis (tremulous iris); **deep anterior chamber**; jet-black (dark) pupil; absence of lens\". This distractor is the one that is diagnostic in reverse. |\n\n**The pair to hold together:** `opqb-t9-405` is the other lens-induced glaucoma — same family, opposite maturity, opposite mechanism. Swollen and immature closes the angle by pushing; liquefied and hypermature clogs an open angle with protein.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Months of untreated cataract followed by a sudden painful stony-hard red eye is phacomorphic glaucoma: an intumescent lens thickens, blocks the pupil and closes the angle, and the treatment is urgent lens extraction.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.61 (Q404), key p.63' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-405', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'**Phacolytic glaucoma** occurs in:',
    options:['Congenital cataract.','Early cortical cataract.','Subluxation of the lens.','Hyper mature cortical cataract.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"*(Secondary chapter: `op-glauc` — a secondary open-angle glaucoma; filed here because the lens is both the cause and the treatment.)*\n\n**Phacolytic glaucoma needs a lens whose cortex has liquefied and whose capsule has begun to fail — and that is the definition of hypermaturity.** `L18) Cataract II`, maturity table, hypermature row: \"**SHRUNKEN type**: cortex leaks → wrinkled capsule. **MORGAGNIAN type**: liquefied cortex, nucleus sinks inferiorly (pathognomonic pseudohypopyon-like appearance). **Risk: phacolytic glaucoma**.\" Its complications table repeats it: \"**Phacolytic Glaucoma** | **Hypermature/mature** | **Liquefied cortex leaks proteins** | HM, pain, redness | **Macrophages block TM** | **Urgent lens removal**.\"\n\n**The mechanism, which `L17) Cataract I` prints in two limbs:** \"**Degeneration of lens capsule → leakage of lens proteins** → immune reaction in anterior chamber (**uveitis**) → **uveitic glaucoma**\" and \"**clogging of TM by lens proteins macromolecules** → **secondary open angle glaucoma (trabecular)**.\" The angle is wide open; what obstructs it is protein and the macrophages that have swallowed it. That is the contrast with `opqb-t9-404`, where the angle is mechanically shut by a swollen lens, and `opqb-t9-351`, which asks how a phacolytic eye is treated — by taking out the lens that is leaking, not by cutting a new drain.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Congenital cataract.** | A child's lens is soft and fluid throughout — `L17`: \"Young age → fluid\" — and has no liquefying cortex behind a degenerating capsule. Its danger is developmental: `L18` gives paediatric cataract \"VA loss + **amblyopia risk**\" and makes a dense congenital cataract an urgent operation \"to **prevent amblyopia** (within weeks)\". |\n| **Early cortical cataract.** | The incipient stage — `L18`: \"VA 6/18–6/6 (mild or none) · Red reflex: clear centrally, dark periphery · **Spoke-like cortical opacities at periphery; minimal visual complaint**.\" The capsule is intact and nothing is leaking. Cortical liquefaction is precisely what has *not* happened yet. |\n| **Subluxation of the lens.** | A **zonular** problem, not a capsular one. A partly dislocated lens can raise the pressure — `L18` makes \"**Subluxated lens with acute pupillary block**\" an urgent indication for surgery — but the mechanism is mechanical obstruction of the pupil, not protein in the trabecular meshwork. Different glaucoma, different name. |\n\n**⚠️ Spelling, for anyone grepping this topic:** the bank prints **\"Phacolytic\"** here and **\"Phakolytic\"** at `opqb-t9-351`. Same disease, two spellings, five pages apart.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Phacolytic glaucoma is a secondary open-angle glaucoma of the hypermature cortical cataract: a degenerating capsule leaks liquefied cortical protein that clogs the trabecular meshwork, and the lens must come out.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.61 (Q405), key p.63' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-406', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'A **taxi driver 60 years old** complaining of **Rt. gradual painless diminution of vision**. The best corrected visual acuity in his **left eye was 5/60** and his **right eye was 6/9**. The most common cause is:',
    options:['Change of refraction.','Vitreous hemorrhage.','Senile cataract changes.','Anterior uveitis.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**⚠️ THE QUESTION IS INTERNALLY INCONSISTENT AS PRINTED, AND IT IS TRANSCRIBED EXACTLY.** The complaint is of the **right** eye; the acuities as printed give the **left** eye **5/60** and the **right** eye **6/9**, which is a nearly normal right eye and a badly reduced left one. The bank prints no correction and the key is unaffected — the answer is the same whichever eye is meant — so the key is recorded as printed and the discrepancy is noted here rather than repaired. It matters only if you try to read the laterality as a clue; the phrase that carries the answer is *gradual painless diminution of vision* in a 60-year-old.\n\n**That phrase is the deck's own headline sentence for cataract, twice over.** `L17) Cataract I`, clinical picture: \"Symptoms (\\\"**Gradual progressive painless diminution of vision**\\\")\", and its take-home message: \"**Cataract is the most common cause of gradual progressive painless diminution of vision**.\" The stem asks for the **most common** cause and the deck answers the question in those words. Its epidemiology slide supplies the age: \"**Senile cataract starts from the 5th decade onwards**, with no gender or laterality predilection\", and \"**Senile cataract is the most common cause of reversible blindness in the world**.\"\n\n**Reading 5/60.** The notation is not a typo. `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination`, slide 120: \"**Reduce the distance** between the patient and the chart, **1 meter at a time**, until the top letter can be seen — the VA will then be recorded as **5/60 or 4/60**, etc.\" So 5/60 means the 60-metre line read at 5 metres. Note also that 5/60 is well above the floor a cataract may reach: `L17` \"Mature cataract → HM · **Never PL or No PL** due to cataract or any media opacity.\"\n\n**Why \"best corrected\" is the word that kills option A.** A best-corrected acuity is measured *after* the refraction has been done and the best lens put in front of the eye. Whatever is reducing this vision has already survived the correction, so by definition it is not a change of refraction. `opqb-t9-396` makes the same point through the pinhole instead.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Change of refraction.** | Excluded by *best corrected*, as above. Refractive change is also a real feature of the ageing lens — `L18) Cataract II` gives nuclear sclerosis an \"**acquired myopia**\" — but that is the cataract, and it is correctable until the opacity itself takes over. |\n| **Vitreous hemorrhage.** | Wrong tempo and wrong symptom. A vitreous haemorrhage is **sudden**, and it announces itself with floaters and a sudden shower or a red haze — not months of gradual dimming. `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` places it in **proliferative** disease, which this stem gives no ground for. |\n| **Anterior uveitis.** | **Painful and red.** `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` builds anterior uveitis on pain, redness, photophobia and keratic precipitates; the stem says *painless*, which excludes it in one word. |\n\n**The professional detail worth keeping:** the patient is a **taxi driver**, and `L18` lists among the elective indications for surgery \"**VA below driving standard** (typically <6/12 in the driving eye)\" and \"Visual impairment affecting quality of life (**driving**, reading, ADL)\". Whichever eye the 5/60 belongs to, this man's living is the reason the operation gets scheduled.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Gradual painless diminution of vision in a 60-year-old is senile cataract — the commonest cause of that presentation and of reversible blindness worldwide; a best-corrected acuity has already excluded refractive change.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.61 (Q406), key p.63' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-407', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'A **60-year-old patient originally emmetropic** came complaining of **defective far vision**, with **good ability to read without his presbyopic correction**. The expected cause is:',
    options:['Keratoconus.','Open angle glaucoma.','Cortical cataract,','Nuclear cataract.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**This is \"second sight\", and `L18) Cataract II` names it in the symptom list of senile cataract:** \"**'Second sight'** — Nuclear: **myopic shift allows reading without glasses (temporary)**\", set beside the nuclear panel's \"**Increased nuclear refractive index → acquired myopia**\" and \"**Interferes predominantly with distant vision**\", with the margin note \"**:nuclear cataract → myopia, not hyperopia**\". Its revision page puts the same fact as a question: \"Q: Nuclear cataract → which vision affected most? A: **DISTANT vision (myopic shift)**. Patient may read better ('second sight')\".\n\n**`opqb-t9-398` carries this topic's full account of the mechanism and of why the improvement is temporary; it is not repeated here.** In one sentence: a denser nucleus is a stronger plus lens, the eye becomes myopic, and a myopic eye of about −2 D reads at arm's length without help — so the presbyopic add becomes unnecessary at exactly the moment distance vision starts to go.\n\n**Why the bank prints this four times.** `opqb-t9-360` asks the mechanism, `opqb-t9-398` and this entry ask the vignette, `opqb-t9-402` asks it as acquired simple myopia. The staging record notes that Q398 and Q407 share the idea but **no wording**, so they are siblings rather than a reprint — and note that the same key sits at letter **C** in Q398 and letter **D** here.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Keratoconus.** | Also causes acquired myopia, but it is a corneal ectasia whose myopia is **irregular and astigmatic**, and it does not begin at 60. It would not hand the patient a clean unaided near acuity. |\n| **Open angle glaucoma.** | Painless and gradual, so it survives the first filter — but it takes the **peripheral field** and leaves central acuity intact until late, and it does nothing whatever to the refraction. Nothing about it would let a presbyope drop his reading glasses. |\n| **Cortical cataract,** | *(Printed with a trailing comma; kept as it is.)* Wrong morphology for this symptom. `L18`: cortical opacities are \"**wedge-shaped / spoke-like peripheral**\", \"appear dark against the red reflex\", \"**Glare prominent** when opacities are equatorial\", and their effect on acuity \"depends on **proximity to the visual axis**\". They scatter light; they do not raise the axial refractive index, so there is no myopic shift and no second sight. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Second sight — a presbyope reading unaided while distance vision fails — is the myopic shift of nuclear cataract, and it is temporary.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.62 (Q407), key p.63' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-408', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'The **field of vision** in a patient with **mature senile cataract** is tested by:',
    options:['Confrontation method.','Perception of light.','Projection of light','Perimetry.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"Visual field testing in mature cataract relies on light perception/projection.\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**⚠️ THE KEY IS PRINTED AS B AND IS RECORDED AS PRINTED, BUT THE DECKS PUT THE FIELD TEST FOR THIS EYE AT OPTION C, AND THE BANK'S OWN BOX NAMES BOTH.** The box says \"light **perception/projection**\" — it does not choose between them — while option **B** is *perception* alone and option **C** is *projection* alone. The discrepancy is recorded here and the key is not disputed.\n\n**What the decks print.** `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination`, slide 123, is a branching diagram of visual field testing and it assigns the methods by acuity:\n\n| Method | The deck's own threshold |\n|---|---|\n| **Confrontation Method** | **VA = 1/60 or better** |\n| **Light Projection** | **VA less than 1/60** |\n| **Amsler Grid** | for the central 10° of the visual field |\n| **Perimetry** (manual, automated) | *(no threshold printed)* |\n\nA mature cataract's acuity is **hand movements** — `L18) Cataract II`, maturity table: \"Mature — VA: **HM only (not less!)** · Red Reflex: **ABSENT**\", and `L17) Cataract I`: \"Mature cataract → HM · **Never PL or No PL** due to cataract or any media opacity.\" HM is far below 1/60, so the deck's own tree lands on **Light Projection**. `L18` says the same thing three separate times: in the signs panel, \"**Projection of light** — **Good in all 4 directions = good prognosis**\"; in the pre-operative checklist, \"**Projection of light in all 4 directions (dense cataract)**\"; and in the clinical approach, \"Visual fields: confrontation · **Light projection (all 4 quadrants) — dense cataract**\".\n\n**What light projection actually is, and why it is a field test at all.** `L1,2`, slide 130: \"Using the **penlight**, with the patient properly occluding the other eye. Light is directed at the eye tested **from different quadrants (superior, inferior, temporal, and nasal)** and the patient is asked to **determine the direction of illumination**. Results are recorded as **Good light projection (GLP)** / **Bad light projection (BLP)**.\" Testing four quadrants separately *is* a crude four-point field: a retina detached inferiorly will fail the superior light, because the field is inverted relative to the retina. That directional information is exactly what the printed key's *perception* of light does **not** give.\n\n**Where perception of light belongs.** It is a rung on the **acuity** ladder, not a field method. `L1,2`, slide 121: \"If the patient cannot detect hand motion: **Shine a torch toward the eye and ask if they can see the light.** If they can, record '**perception of light**' (VA = PL).\" It answers *is there any vision at all*, with no direction attached — and by `L17`'s rule a cataract alone should never take an eye down to PL, so an eye whose best answer is PL has a second disease.\n\n**Why the remaining two are wrong regardless of how the B/C dispute is settled:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Confrontation method.** | Requires the patient to **fixate on the examiner's eye** and count fingers or detect movement in the periphery. `L1,2` sets its floor at **1/60 or better**; a mature cataract at HM cannot fixate a target or resolve a finger, so the test cannot be performed. Note that `L18` still lists \"**Normal VF by confrontation**\" among the signs of senile cataract — that is the *immature* eye, where it demonstrates that the field is full and the problem is purely the media. |\n| **Perimetry.** | Manual or automated perimetry needs steady fixation and the resolution to detect a small dim stimulus against a lit bowl. A dense media opacity depresses the whole field non-specifically and makes the printout uninterpretable, quite apart from the patient being unable to fixate. This bank makes the same point at `opqb-t9-385`, where perimetry is a distractor against electrophysiology in an eye with hand-motion acuity. |\n\n**The principle behind all of it:** in an eye you cannot see into, every pre-operative test is chosen for working **through** an opaque lens — light projection, colour discrimination, B-scan (`opqb-t9-376`) and ERG (`opqb-t9-385`). The next question, `opqb-t9-409`, asks what a bad result on this one means.",
    objective:'A mature cataract sees only hand movements, far below the 1/60 floor for confrontation, so its field is mapped by directing light from the four quadrants — the bank keys perception of light while its own box and the decks name projection.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.62 (Q408), key p.63 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t9-409', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'A **bad prognosis** for visual improvement is expected for **cataract extraction** if:',
    options:['The patient is myopic.','The cataract is hypermature.','The ocular tension is slightly high.','The light projection is bad.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"Poor light projection indicates severely compromised retinal function → bad prognosis for cataract surgery.pt not gets benefit from surgery\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim — including the run-together \"surgery.pt\" and the missing final stop. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**Cataract surgery restores the media. It cannot restore a retina.** Everything in the pre-operative assessment of a dense cataract exists to answer one question — *is there a working retina behind this lens?* — and light projection is the cheapest way to ask it.\n\n**The deck states the rule positively; the bank asks for its negative.** `L18) Cataract II`, senile cataract signs: \"**Projection of light** — **Good in all 4 directions = good prognosis**.\" Its maturity table repeats it for the mature lens: \"Totally opaque white lens. Shadow test: NEGATIVE. **Good projection + colour discrimination = good prognosis**.\" And its pre-operative checklist lists among the visual function tests \"**Projection of light in all 4 directions (dense cataract)**\" and \"**Color discrimination (red vs green)**\", beside \"**Pupillary reflexes (relative afferent pupillary defect?)**\" and \"**Potential acuity measurement (PAM)** if doubt about macular function\". `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination`, slide 130, gives the recording: \"**Good light projection (GLP)** / **Bad light projection (BLP)**.\"\n\n**Why bad projection is so ominous.** The test bypasses the opacity entirely — it needs no image, only light reaching photoreceptors and a retina that can say where it came from. An eye that cannot locate a bright torch in a given quadrant has a retina that is not functioning in that quadrant, and removing the lens will not change that. It is the one pre-operative finding that predicts an operation the patient does not benefit from, which is what the bank's box says in its own blunt words. When projection is bad, or the fundus cannot be seen at all, the deck escalates: \"**B-scan ultrasound if fundus not visible → rule out retinal detachment / tumour**\" (`opqb-t9-376`) and \"**Electroretinogram (ERG) if macular disease suspected**\" (`opqb-t9-385`). `opqb-t9-408` asks how this same test is performed.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — each is a real consideration, and none of them predicts a poor *visual* result:**\n\n| Option | What it actually changes |\n|---|---|\n| **The patient is myopic.** | It changes the **arithmetic**, not the prognosis. `L18` builds IOL power from \"**Axial length** (IOL Master/Lenstar preferred)\" and \"**Keratometry**\", and warns that outlier eyes need the right formula: \"Formulae: SRK/T (standard), **Haigis, Holladay (long/short eyes)**\" — a long myopic eye is precisely such an outlier, and getting it wrong produces a **refractive surprise**, which `L18` treats as correctable (\"IOL exchange or piggyback IOL, LASIK enhancement\"). High myopia is separately listed by `L17) Cataract I` among the **local ocular** causes of complicated cataract. Neither is a bad visual prognosis; a myopic eye with a good projection and a healthy macula does well. |\n| **The cataract is hypermature.** | Being further along the maturity scale says nothing about the retina behind it, and hypermaturity makes surgery **more** urgent rather than less: `L18` gives the hypermature lens \"**Risk: phacolytic glaucoma**\" and its complications table prescribes \"**Urgent lens removal**\". The deck adds only a caution about assessment — \"**OCT when possible in hypermature cataract**\". A hypermature cataract with good projection is expected to do well. |\n| **The ocular tension is slightly high.** | A finding to investigate before operating, not a prediction of failure. What would darken the prognosis is established **glaucomatous damage** — a cupped disc and a lost field — and \"slightly high\" states neither. `L18`'s pre-operative assessment asks instead about \"prior uveitis, trauma, **glaucoma drops**\" as history, and lists \"**Raised IOP** (transient or glaucoma)\" among post-operative complications, not pre-operative contraindications. |\n\n**The sentence to keep:** the lens is the only part of this operation the surgeon controls; the projection test is where the retina votes first.",
    objective:'Bad light projection means the retina itself is not working, and no cataract extraction can repair that — it is the pre-operative finding that predicts no visual benefit from surgery.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.62 (Q409), key p.63 (explanation box printed)' }

